// 영어 변형 문항(1 쉽게·짧게, 2 원문 수준, 3 어렵게). 지문은 모두 새로 쓴 것. 변형/2026_10월학평/bank*.py에서 생성
window.VARS={"en_2026_10월학력평가_18": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 목적으로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Dear Ms. Parker,</p><p>Thank you for choosing Green Petal Flowers for your daughter's wedding. We were excited to prepare the white roses you ordered for next Saturday. Sadly, our main supplier told us yesterday that a storm has damaged most of their rose fields. Because of this, we will not be able to deliver the white roses on the wedding day. We are very sorry for this news. Please call us if you have any questions.</p><p>Sincerely,<br>Green Petal Flowers</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 꽃 배달 날짜 변경을 요청하려고</span><span>② 결혼식 장식 서비스를 홍보하려고</span><span>③ 주문한 흰 장미를 배달할 수 없음을 알리려고</span><span>④ 꽃 가격 인상의 이유를 설명하려고</span><span>⑤ 새로운 꽃 공급업체를 소개하려고</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③ 주문한 흰 장미를 배달할 수 없음을 알리려고", "sol": "폭풍으로 공급처의 장미밭이 피해를 입어 결혼식 날 흰 장미를 배달할 수 없다고 알리는 편지이다. 날짜 변경 요청이나 가격 이야기는 없다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.083, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 목적으로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Dear Members,</p><p>On behalf of the Riverside Community Center, I want to thank all of you who signed up for this autumn's Beginner Pottery Class. Your enthusiasm made it one of our most popular programs this year. Unfortunately, our instructor, Ms. Holt, injured her wrist last week, and her doctor has advised her not to work for at least two months. We searched for a replacement, but we could not find a qualified potter who is available on Tuesday evenings. As a result, we have no choice but to cancel the class for this season. We understand how disappointing this must be, and we will contact each of you separately about your registration fee. We hope to offer the class again next spring.</p><p>Best regards,<br>Laura Kim, Program Director</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 도자기 강좌 강사를 모집하려고</span><span>② 도자기 강좌 일정 변경을 안내하려고</span><span>③ 봄 학기 강좌 신청 방법을 설명하려고</span><span>④ 이번 가을 도자기 강좌가 취소되었음을 알리려고</span><span>⑤ 강좌 등록비 환불 절차를 자세히 안내하려고</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④ 이번 가을 도자기 강좌가 취소되었음을 알리려고", "sol": "강사의 부상으로 대체 강사를 구하지 못해 이번 시즌 도자기 강좌를 취소한다는 공지이다. 등록비는 따로 연락한다고만 했으므로 환불 절차 안내(⑤)가 목적은 아니다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.135, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 목적으로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Dear Mr. Alvarez,</p><p>It has been a privilege to display your grandfather's landscape painting, Morning over Lake Tana, in our gallery since March. Visitors have lingered in front of it more than any other piece in the collection, and several art historians have asked to study it more closely. Under our loan agreement, the painting was to be returned to you on September 30. However, the conservation team recently discovered that the canvas reacts poorly to rapid changes in humidity, and they have strongly recommended that it not be moved until the cooler, drier season begins. Transporting it now could cause small cracks that would be difficult to repair. With your permission, we would therefore keep the work in our climate-controlled storage room, where it will no longer be on view, and deliver it to your home in early December. All related costs, including insurance, will of course be covered by the museum.</p><p>With sincere gratitude,<br>Helen Ward, Chief Curator</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 전시 기간 연장에 따른 추가 관람을 홍보하려고</span><span>② 작품 보존 처리 비용의 분담을 요청하려고</span><span>③ 대여받은 그림의 반환을 늦추는 것에 대해 양해를 구하려고</span><span>④ 미술사학자들의 그림 연구 참여를 제안하려고</span><span>⑤ 그림을 박물관에 기증해 줄 것을 요청하려고</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③ 대여받은 그림의 반환을 늦추는 것에 대해 양해를 구하려고", "sol": "9월 30일에 돌려주기로 한 그림을 습도 문제로 12월 초에 돌려주겠다며 허락을 구하는 편지이다. 그림은 전시하지 않고 보관한다고 했으므로 ①은 틀리고, 비용은 박물관이 모두 낸다고 했으므로 ②도 틀리다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.45, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_19": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글에 드러난 Jiho의 심경 변화로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Jiho stood behind the curtain, waiting for his turn in the speech contest. His hands were cold, and he kept reading his notes again and again. \"What if I forget everything?\" he thought. When his name was called, he walked slowly to the stage. But as he began to speak, the audience nodded and smiled. His voice became stronger, and the words came easily. When he finished, the room filled with loud applause. Jiho let out a long breath and smiled. It was over, and it had gone well.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① nervous → relieved</span><span>② bored → excited</span><span>③ proud → ashamed</span><span>④ calm → annoyed</span><span>⑤ hopeful → disappointed</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "1", "anst": "① nervous → relieved", "sol": "대회 전 손이 차갑고 원고를 계속 읽으며 걱정하다가(nervous), 발표가 잘 끝나고 숨을 내쉬며 웃는다(relieved).", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.083, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글에 드러난 Nora의 심경 변화로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Nora had been looking forward to this moment for months. She had saved every coin from her part-time job to buy the vintage camera she had seen in the shop window on Elm Street. On Saturday morning, she hurried there with the money in an envelope, imagining the first photos she would take. She pushed open the door and looked toward the window display. The shelf was empty. \"I'm sorry,\" said the shop owner. \"A collector bought it yesterday afternoon.\" Nora stood still for a long time, holding the envelope. She thanked the owner in a small voice and walked out into the gray street, her shoulders dropping with every step.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① curious → indifferent</span><span>② eager → disheartened</span><span>③ worried → confident</span><span>④ jealous → grateful</span><span>⑤ calm → frightened</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "② eager → disheartened", "sol": "몇 달 동안 돈을 모아 카메라를 사러 서둘러 가며 기대했지만(eager), 이미 팔렸다는 말을 듣고 작은 목소리로 인사한 뒤 어깨가 처진 채 나간다(disheartened).", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.135, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글에 드러난 Ellen의 심경 변화로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>The waiting room smelled of coffee that no one was drinking. Ellen sat with her portfolio on her knees, counting the floor tiles for the third time. Each candidate who came out of the interview room avoided her eyes, and she read in their faces a story she did not want to hear. When the door finally opened for her, the panel barely looked up. The first question was about a project she had abandoned two years earlier. She answered, but her voice sounded thin even to herself. Then the oldest interviewer turned a page in her portfolio and stopped. \"You designed this bridge model?\" he asked, leaning forward. For the next twenty minutes, the conversation was not an examination but an exchange between people who loved the same thing. When Ellen stepped back into the waiting room, she noticed for the first time that the window looked out over the river, and she found herself humming.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① tense → elated</span><span>② confident → embarrassed</span><span>③ indifferent → curious</span><span>④ excited → regretful</span><span>⑤ sorrowful → sympathetic</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "1", "anst": "① tense → elated", "sol": "면접 전 바닥 타일을 세며 다른 지원자들 표정에 불안해하고 목소리도 가늘었지만(tense), 자신의 다리 모형에 관심을 받고 대화를 나눈 뒤 콧노래를 부른다(elated). 감정 낱말 없이 행동으로 드러나므로 단서를 모아 판단해야 한다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.45, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_20": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글에서 필자가 주장하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Many students stay quiet in class because they are afraid of asking a \"stupid\" question. But there is no such thing as a stupid question when you are learning. If you do not understand something, other students probably feel the same way. When you raise your hand, you help yourself and your classmates. Teachers also want to know which parts are difficult so that they can explain them better. So, the next time you are confused, do not hide it. Ask your question bravely.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 수업 전에 교과서를 미리 읽어야 한다.</span><span>② 모르는 것이 있으면 두려워하지 말고 질문해야 한다.</span><span>③ 친구의 질문에 친절하게 답해 주어야 한다.</span><span>④ 교사는 학생의 수준에 맞게 설명해야 한다.</span><span>⑤ 어려운 내용은 혼자 힘으로 해결해야 한다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "② 모르는 것이 있으면 두려워하지 말고 질문해야 한다.", "sol": "어리석은 질문은 없으며, 모르면 숨기지 말고 용기 있게 질문하라는 주장이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.083, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글에서 필자가 주장하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>We often decide what we think of a person within the first few seconds of meeting them. A firm handshake suggests confidence; a quiet voice suggests shyness. These quick judgments feel natural, and sometimes they are even correct. The problem is that once we have formed an impression, we tend to notice only the information that supports it and ignore everything else. The quiet new classmate may turn out to be the funniest person you know, but only if you give her the chance to show it. Before you decide who someone is, spend time with them in different situations. Listen more than you label. Treat your first impression as a rough draft, not a final version, and be willing to rewrite it as you learn more.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 자신의 첫인상을 좋게 관리해야 한다.</span><span>② 처음 만난 사람에게 먼저 다가가야 한다.</span><span>③ 첫인상에 기대어 사람을 섣불리 판단하지 말아야 한다.</span><span>④ 상대방의 말을 끝까지 들은 뒤에 말해야 한다.</span><span>⑤ 다양한 상황에서 자신의 모습을 보여 주어야 한다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③ 첫인상에 기대어 사람을 섣불리 판단하지 말아야 한다.", "sol": "첫인상은 굳어지면 그것을 뒷받침하는 정보만 보게 되므로, 첫인상을 초안으로 여기고 사람을 섣불리 규정하지 말라는 주장이다. ①·⑤는 자신을 보여 주는 쪽의 이야기라 방향이 반대이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.135, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글에서 필자가 주장하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>A headline that confirms what we already believe slips past our defenses almost without a sound. We share it, quote it, and build arguments on it, rarely pausing to ask where it came from. Yet the speed at which information now travels has made the source of a claim more important, not less. A statistic stripped of its original study, a quotation separated from its speaker, or a photograph detached from its date can be made to say nearly anything. The habit worth cultivating, then, is not general suspicion — that leads only to cynicism, in which nothing is believed and nothing is learned — but a specific curiosity about origins. Who first said this? Based on what evidence? What would they gain if I believed it? Asking these questions takes a few minutes, but those minutes are the price of holding opinions that are genuinely our own rather than borrowed from whoever spoke loudest.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 모든 정보를 의심하는 태도로 비판적 사고를 길러야 한다.</span><span>② 자신과 다른 의견을 담은 기사도 균형 있게 읽어야 한다.</span><span>③ 정보를 빠르게 공유하기보다 정확하게 기록해야 한다.</span><span>④ 정보를 받아들일 때 그 출처와 근거를 따져 보아야 한다.</span><span>⑤ 전문가의 의견보다 자신의 판단을 더 신뢰해야 한다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④ 정보를 받아들일 때 그 출처와 근거를 따져 보아야 한다.", "sol": "정보가 빨리 퍼질수록 출처가 더 중요하므로, 누가 어떤 근거로 말했는지 따지는 습관을 기르라는 주장이다. 무조건 의심하는 태도(①)는 냉소로 이어진다며 오히려 부정한다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.45, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_21": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>밑줄 친 plants seeds rather than builds walls가 다음 글에서 의미하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Some coaches control every move their players make. They decide where each player should stand, when to pass, and when to shoot. Players under these coaches often follow rules well, but they rarely learn to think for themselves. Good coaches work differently. They give players a few basic ideas and then let them try, fail, and find their own way. Over time, the players grow into creative athletes who can make smart decisions in a real game. In this sense, a good coach <u>plants seeds rather than builds walls</u>.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① gives players strict rules to protect them from mistakes</span><span>② helps players develop their own abilities instead of limiting them</span><span>③ chooses only the most talented players for the team</span><span>④ spends more time on physical training than on strategy</span><span>⑤ separates strong players from weak players during practice</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "② helps players develop their own abilities instead of limiting them", "sol": "좋은 코치는 몇 가지 기본만 주고 선수가 스스로 시도하고 실패하며 성장하게 한다. \"씨앗을 심는다\"는 성장을 돕는 것, \"벽을 쌓는다\"는 통제하고 제한하는 것을 뜻한다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.361, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>밑줄 친 an invisible gardener가 다음 글에서 의미하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Readers rarely think about editors. When a novel moves smoothly from one chapter to the next, when a character's voice stays consistent, or when a confusing paragraph has quietly disappeared, we give the credit to the author. That is exactly how good editors want it. Their job is not to plant new ideas in the writer's field but to cut away what blocks the light, support the stems that are bending, and make sure that what the author planted can grow to its full height. An editor who rewrites a book in her own style has failed, even if the result is beautiful, because the reader is no longer meeting the author. The best editing leaves no fingerprints. In this sense, the editor is <u>an invisible gardener</u> of someone else's work.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① a critic who judges whether a book deserves to be published</span><span>② a co-author who adds creative ideas of his or her own to the story</span><span>③ a helper who strengthens the writer's work without leaving visible traces</span><span>④ a reader who quietly enjoys a book before anyone else</span><span>⑤ a manager who decides when and where a book will be sold</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③ a helper who strengthens the writer's work without leaving visible traces", "sol": "편집자는 새 생각을 심지 않고 방해되는 것을 걷어 내어 작가의 글이 자라게 돕되, 흔적을 남기지 않는다. ②처럼 자기 생각을 더하는 것은 실패한 편집이라고 했다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.59, "d": "mid"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>밑줄 친 repaints the scene each time it is visited가 다음 글에서 의미하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>We like to imagine memory as a storehouse of photographs: an event happens, the mind records it, and later we pull out the print and look at it again. Decades of research suggest a less comfortable picture. Each time we recall an experience, the brain does not retrieve a fixed file; it rebuilds the event from scattered fragments, filling the gaps with what seems plausible given what we know now. Our current mood, a story we heard from a friend, even the wording of a question can slip into the reconstruction, and the revised version is what gets stored for next time. This is why two siblings can hold vivid, sincere, and incompatible memories of the same childhood holiday. Neither is lying; each has simply revisited the past through a different present. Memory, in other words, does not keep the original canvas safe behind glass. It <u>repaints the scene each time it is visited</u>.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① gradually loses details until only the emotional core of an event remains</span><span>② stores separate copies of an event for each person who experienced it</span><span>③ faithfully reproduces past events whenever we focus our attention on them</span><span>④ reconstructs the past under the influence of present knowledge and context</span><span>⑤ replaces painful experiences with more pleasant versions of the same event</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④ reconstructs the past under the influence of present knowledge and context", "sol": "기억은 고정된 사진을 꺼내는 것이 아니라 회상할 때마다 지금의 지식·기분·질문의 표현에 영향을 받아 다시 만들어진다. ①은 세부가 사라진다는 것만, ⑤는 즐거운 쪽으로만 바뀐다는 것이라 글의 핵심(현재의 영향으로 재구성)과 다르다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.804, "d": "hi"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_22": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 요지로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Many students now take notes on laptops because typing is fast. They can write down almost every word the teacher says. But studies show that students who take notes by hand often remember the lesson better. Writing by hand is slower, so students cannot copy everything. Instead, they have to listen carefully, choose the important points, and put them in their own words. This extra thinking helps the information stay in their memory.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 노트북을 사용하면 수업 내용을 빠짐없이 기록할 수 있다.</span><span>② 손으로 필기하면 내용을 정리하며 생각하게 되어 기억에 도움이 된다.</span><span>③ 수업 중에는 필기보다 듣기에 집중하는 것이 좋다.</span><span>④ 필기 방법은 학생의 성향에 맞게 선택해야 한다.</span><span>⑤ 교사는 핵심 내용을 학생들에게 직접 정리해 주어야 한다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "② 손으로 필기하면 내용을 정리하며 생각하게 되어 기억에 도움이 된다.", "sol": "손 필기는 느려서 모두 받아 적을 수 없으므로 중요한 것을 골라 자기 말로 바꾸게 되고, 그 생각 과정이 기억을 돕는다는 내용이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.083, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 요지로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Smartphones have turned all of us into photographers. On a single trip, we may take hundreds of pictures, and cloud storage means we never have to delete any of them. It would seem that we are recording our lives more completely than any generation before us. Yet psychologists have found something surprising. In one experiment, museum visitors who photographed objects later remembered fewer details about them than visitors who simply looked. The camera, it seems, becomes a kind of external memory: once we know the image is saved, the brain feels free to let the experience go. What is more, the sheer number of photos makes it unlikely that we will ever look through them again. The more we capture, the less we may actually keep.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 여행지의 사진은 오래도록 추억을 떠올리게 해 준다.</span><span>② 디지털 사진은 정기적으로 정리하여 보관해야 한다.</span><span>③ 사진을 많이 찍는 것이 경험을 기억하는 데 오히려 방해가 될 수 있다.</span><span>④ 박물관에서는 작품 보호를 위해 사진 촬영을 제한해야 한다.</span><span>⑤ 클라우드 저장 기술이 사진 문화의 확산을 이끌었다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③ 사진을 많이 찍는 것이 경험을 기억하는 데 오히려 방해가 될 수 있다.", "sol": "사진을 찍으면 뇌가 경험을 저장할 필요를 덜 느껴 세부를 덜 기억하고, 사진이 너무 많아 다시 보지도 않는다. 많이 찍을수록 덜 남는다는 것이 요지이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.135, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 요지로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Every few years a new collaboration tool promises to end the misunderstandings that plague teams: shared documents that update in real time, chat channels for every project, video calls that make distance irrelevant. Each tool genuinely removes a barrier. Information that once took days to circulate now reaches everyone in seconds. And yet surveys of office workers report, with remarkable consistency, that unclear expectations and poor coordination remain their greatest sources of frustration. The reason is that most miscommunication was never a problem of transmission. It arises from assumptions people do not know they hold, from priorities that differ but are never stated, and from the reluctance to admit confusion in front of colleagues. Faster channels carry these problems as efficiently as they carry everything else. A team that has not agreed on what \"done\" means will simply disagree about it more quickly.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 협업 도구는 업무 정보 전달 속도를 크게 높여 주었다.</span><span>② 팀원 간 신뢰가 쌓이면 소통 도구가 필요 없어진다.</span><span>③ 원격 근무가 늘수록 대면 회의의 중요성이 커진다.</span><span>④ 새로운 협업 도구는 사용법을 충분히 익힌 뒤 도입해야 한다.</span><span>⑤ 소통 도구가 발전해도 생각의 차이에서 오는 소통 문제는 해결되지 않는다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "5", "anst": "⑤ 소통 도구가 발전해도 생각의 차이에서 오는 소통 문제는 해결되지 않는다.", "sol": "도구는 전달 속도를 높였지만(①은 사실이나 요지 아님), 오해는 전달의 문제가 아니라 드러나지 않은 가정과 우선순위 차이에서 생기므로 도구로 해결되지 않는다는 것이 요지이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.45, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_23": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 주제로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Have you ever noticed that you smile when someone smiles at you? This happens to most people without thinking. Scientists say that our brains naturally copy the facial expressions of others. When we see a happy face, the muscles in our own face move in a similar way. This copying also helps us feel what the other person is feeling. That is why a smile can quickly spread through a room and make everyone feel more friendly.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① why smiles spread from person to person</span><span>② how to make a good first impression</span><span>③ the health benefits of laughing every day</span><span>④ the difference between real and fake smiles</span><span>⑤ ways to hide our true feelings from others</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "1", "anst": "① why smiles spread from person to person", "sol": "뇌가 다른 사람의 표정을 따라 하기 때문에 미소가 사람들 사이에 퍼진다는 내용이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.151, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 주제로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>How close should you stand to someone during a conversation? The answer depends a great deal on where you grew up. In many parts of Latin America and the Middle East, friends often stand close enough to touch, and stepping back may be read as coldness. In much of Northern Europe and East Asia, the same distance could feel uncomfortable, even rude, between people who are not family. Researchers who measured comfortable talking distances in dozens of countries found differences of more than half a meter. Such differences can cause quiet misunderstandings: one person keeps moving forward to seem friendly, while the other keeps backing away to seem polite, and each leaves thinking the other was strange.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① ways to improve communication skills at work</span><span>② cultural differences in comfortable personal distance</span><span>③ the role of touch in building close friendships</span><span>④ why people in cold regions prefer more personal space</span><span>⑤ the effects of eye contact in conversations</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "② cultural differences in comfortable personal distance", "sol": "대화할 때 편안한 거리가 문화마다 다르고, 그 차이가 오해를 낳는다는 내용이다. ④는 기후를 원인으로 들지만 글에 없는 내용이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.247, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 주제로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>In the early weeks of a friendship, people work hard to signal interest. They lean in, mirror each other's posture, laugh a little longer than the joke deserves, and hold eye contact as if to prove they are paying attention. Observers of new couples and new friends find these signals so reliable that they can predict, from a few minutes of silent video, how much two people like each other. What happens later is less often studied but just as revealing. As the relationship settles, the obvious signals fade. Long-term friends may sit side by side without looking at each other, finish a conversation in a few words, or communicate disapproval with nothing more than a slight pause. To an outsider such pairs may look distant, but the economy of their signals reflects confidence rather than coolness: when closeness no longer needs to be demonstrated, it can be expressed in a private shorthand that only the two of them understand.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① the risks of misreading silent signals in new relationships</span><span>② how body language reveals our hidden negative feelings</span><span>③ why close friends tend to argue less over time</span><span>④ the methods researchers use to measure attraction</span><span>⑤ how nonverbal signals of closeness change as relationships mature</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "5", "anst": "⑤ how nonverbal signals of closeness change as relationships mature", "sol": "관계 초기에는 눈에 띄는 비언어 신호를 많이 쓰지만, 관계가 깊어지면 신호가 줄고 둘만의 간결한 방식으로 친밀감을 표현한다는 내용이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.45, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_24": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Some managers think that being kind to workers is a waste of time and money. However, a recent study found the opposite. In companies where managers thanked their workers and listened to their ideas, workers were happier and worked harder. They also stayed at the company longer, so the company spent less money hiring and training new people. Kindness, it turns out, is not just nice. It is also good for business.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① How to Become a Successful Manager</span><span>② Kindness at Work: A Smart Business Choice</span><span>③ Why Workers Leave Their Jobs Early</span><span>④ The Hidden Costs of Training New Workers</span><span>⑤ Hard Work Is the Key to Happiness</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "② Kindness at Work: A Smart Business Choice", "sol": "직원에게 친절한 회사가 생산성도 높고 이직도 적어 비용을 아낀다는 내용으로, 친절이 현명한 경영 선택이라는 제목이 알맞다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.228, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>When a cosmetics company first replaced its plastic containers with glass and paper, many analysts predicted a disaster. The new packaging cost nearly twenty percent more, and the company could not raise its prices without losing customers to cheaper competitors. Two years later, the numbers told a different story. Sales among customers under thirty had risen sharply, and online reviews frequently mentioned the packaging as the reason for choosing the brand. The company also saved money in an unexpected way: several countries introduced taxes on single-use plastics, and its competitors suddenly faced costs that it had already absorbed. What had looked like an expensive gesture of environmental responsibility turned out to be one of the most profitable decisions in the company's history.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① Plastic Taxes: A Burden for Small Businesses</span><span>② Are Young Consumers Really Loyal to Brands?</span><span>③ Going Green: A Costly Choice That Paid Off</span><span>④ Why Glass Is Safer Than Plastic for Cosmetics</span><span>⑤ The Hidden Price of Cheaper Competitors</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③ Going Green: A Costly Choice That Paid Off", "sol": "비싼 친환경 포장이 젊은 고객 증가와 플라스틱세 회피로 결국 큰 이익이 되었다는 내용이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.373, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>For most of the twentieth century, corporate secrecy was treated as a form of prudence. What a firm paid its suppliers, how it calculated prices, and where its products were made were matters to be guarded from competitors and, often, from customers. A growing number of companies are now betting that the opposite approach can be an asset. Some publish the full cost breakdown of each product; others disclose the factories they use and the wages paid there. The motive is not purely altruistic. In markets crowded with nearly identical products, trust is one of the few advantages that cannot be copied overnight, and openness signals that a company has nothing to hide. Transparency also disciplines the firm itself: managers who know their decisions will be visible are less tempted by shortcuts that might later become scandals. Of course, disclosure carries risks, as rivals learn more than they otherwise would. But for these firms, being seen clearly has proved more valuable than being hard to see.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① The Fading Line Between Competitors and Partners</span><span>② Why Corporate Scandals Are Increasing Today</span><span>③ Secrecy: The Last Defense in Crowded Markets</span><span>④ How Fair Wages Shape Factory Productivity</span><span>⑤ Open Books, Open Doors: Transparency as Strategy</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "5", "anst": "⑤ Open Books, Open Doors: Transparency as Strategy", "sol": "원가·공장·임금을 공개하는 투명성이 신뢰라는 복제할 수 없는 장점을 주고 스스로를 단속하게 해 전략적 자산이 된다는 내용이다. ③은 과거의 관점으로 글의 결론과 반대이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.526, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_25": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 표의 내용과 일치하지 않는 것은?</p><div class='psg'><div class='tbl'><div class='tt'>Favorite After-School Activities of Students (2025)</div><table><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Middle School (%)</th><th>High School (%)</th></tr><tr><td>Sports</td><td>35</td><td>22</td></tr><tr><td>Gaming</td><td>28</td><td>25</td></tr><tr><td>Reading</td><td>12</td><td>18</td></tr><tr><td>Music</td><td>15</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>Other</td><td>10</td><td>15</td></tr></table></div><p>The table above shows the favorite after-school activities of middle and high school students in 2025. ① Sports was the most popular activity among middle school students. ② Gaming was the most popular activity among high school students. ③ The percentage of high school students who chose Reading was higher than that of middle school students. ④ Among middle school students, Reading was more popular than Music. ⑤ In both groups, Other was the least popular activity.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④", "sol": "중학생은 Reading 12%, Music 15%로 Music이 더 인기가 많다. 나머지는 표와 맞다(고등학생은 Gaming 25%가 가장 높고, 두 집단 모두 Other가 가장 낮다).", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.083, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 표의 내용과 일치하지 않는 것은?</p><div class='psg'><div class='tbl'><div class='tt'>How Teenagers in Four Countries Get News (2025)</div><table><tr><th>Country</th><th>Social Media (%)</th><th>Video Platforms (%)</th><th>News Websites (%)</th><th>TV (%)</th></tr><tr><td>Korea</td><td>38</td><td>31</td><td>18</td><td>13</td></tr><tr><td>Japan</td><td>29</td><td>22</td><td>21</td><td>28</td></tr><tr><td>UK</td><td>41</td><td>27</td><td>20</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>Brazil</td><td>46</td><td>30</td><td>11</td><td>13</td></tr></table><div class='tn'>Note: Each row adds up to 100%.</div></div><p>The table above shows the main sources teenagers in four countries used to get news in 2025. ① In all four countries, social media was the most common source of news for teenagers. ② The percentage of teenagers who used video platforms was the highest in Korea, followed by Brazil. ③ In Japan, the percentage of teenagers who used TV was more than twice that in the UK. ④ The percentage of teenagers who used news websites was higher in Korea than in the UK, but lower than in Japan. ⑤ In Brazil, the percentage of teenagers who used news websites was the lowest among the four sources.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④", "sol": "뉴스 웹사이트는 한국 18%, 영국 20%로 한국이 영국보다 낮다. ① 네 나라 모두 소셜 미디어가 가장 높다(일본 29%>28%). ② 31>30>27>22. ③ 28>12×2. ⑤ 브라질 11%가 가장 낮다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.135, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 표의 내용과 일치하지 않는 것은?</p><div class='psg'><div class='tbl'><div class='tt'>Household Spending on Streaming Services by Age of Household Head (2023 vs. 2025)</div><table><tr><th>Age Group</th><th>2023 (&#36;/month)</th><th>2025 (&#36;/month)</th><th>Number of Services (2025)</th></tr><tr><td>Under 30</td><td>24</td><td>30</td><td>3.4</td></tr><tr><td>30–44</td><td>29</td><td>33</td><td>3.1</td></tr><tr><td>45–59</td><td>21</td><td>27</td><td>2.6</td></tr><tr><td>60 and over</td><td>12</td><td>20</td><td>1.8</td></tr></table></div><p>The table above shows average monthly household spending on streaming services in 2023 and 2025 and the average number of services used in 2025, by age of household head. ① In both years, households headed by people aged 30–44 spent the most on streaming services. ② From 2023 to 2025, spending increased in all age groups, and the largest increase in dollars was seen in the 60 and over group. ③ In 2025, households headed by people under 30 used more streaming services on average than any other group, although they did not spend the most. ④ The spending of the 45–59 group in 2025 was lower than the spending of the under 30 group in 2023. ⑤ In 2025, the average spending per service was higher for the 30–44 group than for the under 30 group.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④", "sol": "45–59 그룹의 2025년 지출은 27달러로 30세 미만 그룹의 2023년 지출 24달러보다 높으므로 ④가 틀리다. ① 29·33으로 두 해 모두 최고. ② 증가액 6·4·6·8달러로 60세 이상이 가장 크다. ③ 3.4개로 최다, 지출은 30–44가 최다. ⑤ 33÷3.1≈10.6 > 30÷3.4≈8.8.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.45, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_26": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>Marie Curie에 관한 다음 글의 내용과 일치하지 않는 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Marie Curie was a scientist famous for her research on radioactivity. She was born in Warsaw, Poland, in 1867. As a young woman, she moved to Paris and studied physics and mathematics at the Sorbonne. There she met Pierre Curie, and they later married and worked together. In 1903, she shared the Nobel Prize in Physics with Pierre Curie and Henri Becquerel. In 1911, she won a second Nobel Prize, this time in Chemistry. She was the first person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 1867년에 폴란드 바르샤바에서 태어났다.</span><span>② 파리의 소르본에서 물리학과 수학을 공부했다.</span><span>③ 1903년에 노벨 물리학상을 혼자 받았다.</span><span>④ 1911년에 노벨 화학상을 받았다.</span><span>⑤ 서로 다른 두 과학 분야에서 노벨상을 받은 최초의 사람이다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③ 1903년에 노벨 물리학상을 혼자 받았다.", "sol": "1903년 노벨 물리학상은 Pierre Curie, Henri Becquerel과 함께 받았다(shared).", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.083, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>Jane Goodall에 관한 다음 글의 내용과 일치하지 않는 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Jane Goodall is a British researcher best known for her long-term study of wild chimpanzees. Born in London in 1934, she loved animals from an early age and dreamed of going to Africa. After finishing school, she could not afford to attend university, so she worked as a secretary and a waitress to save money for her trip. In Kenya she met the famous scientist Louis Leakey, who later sent her to Gombe in Tanzania in 1960 to observe chimpanzees. There she discovered that chimpanzees make and use tools, an ability once thought to belong only to humans. Although she had no undergraduate degree, she was allowed to study at the University of Cambridge and earned a PhD in 1966. In 1977, she founded the Jane Goodall Institute to support research and the protection of chimpanzees.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 어린 시절부터 동물을 좋아했다.</span><span>② 학교를 마친 뒤 비서와 웨이트리스로 일했다.</span><span>③ Louis Leakey가 그녀를 탄자니아의 Gombe로 보냈다.</span><span>④ 침팬지가 도구를 만들고 사용한다는 것을 발견했다.</span><span>⑤ 대학 학부 학위를 받은 뒤 케임브리지 대학교에서 박사 학위를 받았다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "5", "anst": "⑤ 대학 학부 학위를 받은 뒤 케임브리지 대학교에서 박사 학위를 받았다.", "sol": "학부 학위가 없었는데도(Although she had no undergraduate degree) 케임브리지에서 공부해 박사 학위를 받았다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.135, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>Alan Turing에 관한 다음 글의 내용과 일치하지 않는 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Alan Turing, a British mathematician whose ideas helped lay the foundations of computer science, was born in London in 1912. Although his teachers at school often complained that he neglected subjects other than mathematics and science, he went on to study mathematics at King's College, Cambridge. In 1936, he published a paper describing an imaginary device, now called a Turing machine, which could carry out any calculation that could be expressed as a set of rules. During the Second World War, he worked at Bletchley Park, the British code-breaking center, where he played a leading role in breaking messages encrypted by the German Enigma machine. After the war, he turned to the question of whether machines could think. In a 1950 paper, rather than trying to define thinking directly, he proposed a test, the \"imitation game,\" in which a machine would try to convince a human questioner that it was a person. He died in 1954, but his test is still discussed in debates about artificial intelligence.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 학창 시절 교사들은 그가 수학과 과학 외의 과목을 소홀히 한다고 불평했다.</span><span>② 1936년에 오늘날 튜링 기계라 불리는 장치를 설명한 논문을 발표했다.</span><span>③ 전쟁 중 Bletchley Park에서 Enigma 암호 해독에 주도적인 역할을 했다.</span><span>④ 1950년 논문에서 생각하는 것의 뜻을 직접 정의하는 데 집중했다.</span><span>⑤ 그가 제안한 시험은 인공 지능 논쟁에서 여전히 다루어진다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④ 1950년 논문에서 생각하는 것의 뜻을 직접 정의하는 데 집중했다.", "sol": "1950년 논문에서 생각을 직접 정의하는 대신(rather than trying to define thinking directly) 모방 게임이라는 시험을 제안했다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.45, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_27": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>Spring Book Swap에 관한 다음 안내문의 내용과 일치하지 않는 것은?</p><div class='psg'><div class='box'><b>Spring Book Swap</b><br>Bring a book, take a book!<br>■ Date & Place<br>- Saturday, April 12, 10 a.m. – 2 p.m.<br>- Maple Town Library, first floor<br>■ How It Works<br>- Bring up to 5 used books in good condition.<br>- Get one ticket for each book you bring.<br>- Use your tickets to choose new books.<br>■ Notes<br>- Comic books are welcome.<br>- Free snacks will be provided.</div></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 4월 12일 토요일에 열린다.</span><span>② 도서관 1층에서 열린다.</span><span>③ 헌책을 최대 5권까지 가져올 수 있다.</span><span>④ 가져온 책 한 권마다 표를 한 장 받는다.</span><span>⑤ 만화책은 가져올 수 없다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "5", "anst": "⑤ 만화책은 가져올 수 없다.", "sol": "만화책도 환영한다(Comic books are welcome).", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.083, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>Night Sky Photography Workshop에 관한 다음 안내문의 내용과 일치하지 않는 것은?</p><div class='psg'><div class='box'><b>Night Sky Photography Workshop</b><br>Learn to capture the stars with your own camera!<br>■ When & Where<br>- Friday, October 17, 8:00 p.m. – 11:00 p.m.<br>- Hilltop Observatory Parking Lot<br>■ Who Can Join<br>- Anyone aged 14 or older (participants under 18 must come with an adult)<br>- Limited to 20 people<br>■ What to Bring<br>- A camera that allows manual settings (smartphones not accepted)<br>- A tripod (a limited number can be borrowed on site)<br>■ Fee<br>- &#36;30 per person (hot drinks included)<br>※ If it rains, the workshop will be moved to Saturday, October 18.</div></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 14세 이상이면 누구나 참가할 수 있다.</span><span>② 참가 인원은 20명으로 제한된다.</span><span>③ 스마트폰으로도 참가할 수 있다.</span><span>④ 삼각대는 현장에서 일부 빌릴 수 있다.</span><span>⑤ 비가 오면 다음 날로 미뤄진다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③ 스마트폰으로도 참가할 수 있다.", "sol": "수동 설정이 되는 카메라가 필요하고 스마트폰은 받지 않는다(smartphones not accepted).", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.135, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>Riverside Community Garden Plots에 관한 다음 안내문의 내용과 일치하지 않는 것은?</p><div class='psg'><div class='box'><b>Riverside Community Garden Plots 2026</b><br>■ Application Period<br>- March 2 – March 13 (online only)<br>- Plots will be assigned by lottery on March 16; results will be sent by text message.<br>■ Eligibility<br>- Residents of Riverside District (proof of address required)<br>- One plot per household<br>■ Plot Size & Fees<br>- Standard plot (3 m × 4 m): &#36;40 for the season<br>- Raised bed for wheelchair users (1 m × 3 m): free of charge<br>■ Rules<br>- Plots must be planted by April 30; unplanted plots will be given to the next person on the waiting list.<br>- Chemical pesticides are not allowed.<br>- Tools are provided in the shared shed, but must be returned the same day.</div></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 신청은 온라인으로만 받는다.</span><span>② 추첨 결과는 문자 메시지로 보낸다.</span><span>③ 한 가구당 한 구획만 배정된다.</span><span>④ 휠체어 사용자를 위한 화단은 무료이다.</span><span>⑤ 공용 창고의 도구는 다음 날까지 반납하면 된다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "5", "anst": "⑤ 공용 창고의 도구는 다음 날까지 반납하면 된다.", "sol": "도구는 같은 날 반납해야 한다(must be returned the same day).", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.45, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_28": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>Kids Cooking Class에 관한 다음 안내문의 내용과 일치하는 것은?</p><div class='psg'><div class='box'><b>Kids Cooking Class</b><br>Let's make pizza together!<br>■ Date & Time<br>- Every Sunday in May, 11 a.m. – 12:30 p.m.<br>■ Who Can Join<br>- Children aged 7 to 12<br>■ Fee<br>- &#36;15 per class (all ingredients included)<br>■ Notes<br>- Please bring an apron.<br>- Parents can watch the class from the waiting room.</div></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 5월 매주 토요일에 열린다.</span><span>② 13세 어린이도 참가할 수 있다.</span><span>③ 수업료에 재료비가 포함되어 있다.</span><span>④ 앞치마는 무료로 나누어 준다.</span><span>⑤ 부모는 수업에 함께 참여해야 한다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③ 수업료에 재료비가 포함되어 있다.", "sol": "수업료 15달러에 모든 재료가 포함된다. 일요일에 열리고, 7~12세 대상이며, 앞치마는 가져와야 하고, 부모는 대기실에서 볼 수 있다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.083, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>Harbor Kayak Tour에 관한 다음 안내문의 내용과 일치하는 것은?</p><div class='psg'><div class='box'><b>Harbor Kayak Tour</b><br>See the city from the water!<br>■ Schedule<br>- Tuesdays and Thursdays, 9 a.m. and 2 p.m. (2 hours each)<br>- Meeting point: Pier 7 Boathouse<br>■ Requirements<br>- Ages 12 and up (under 16 must share a kayak with an adult)<br>- Basic swimming ability required<br>■ Price<br>- Single kayak: &#36;40 / Double kayak: &#36;65<br>- Life jackets and waterproof bags are provided.<br>■ Cancellation<br>- Full refund if canceled 24 hours in advance<br>- Tours may be canceled due to strong winds; in this case, you can choose a refund or another date.</div></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 투어는 주말에만 운영된다.</span><span>② 15세 참가자는 혼자 카약을 탈 수 있다.</span><span>③ 구명조끼는 직접 가져와야 한다.</span><span>④ 하루 전에 취소하면 전액 환불받을 수 있다.</span><span>⑤ 강풍으로 취소되면 다른 날짜로만 바꿀 수 있다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④ 하루 전에 취소하면 전액 환불받을 수 있다.", "sol": "24시간 전에 취소하면 전액 환불된다. 화·목요일 운영, 16세 미만은 어른과 함께 타야 하고, 구명조끼는 제공되며, 강풍 취소 시 환불이나 다른 날짜 중 고를 수 있다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.135, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>Youth Science Fair 2026에 관한 다음 안내문의 내용과 일치하는 것은?</p><div class='psg'><div class='box'><b>Youth Science Fair 2026</b><br>■ Eligibility<br>- High school students in grades 10–12<br>- Individual entries or teams of up to 3 students<br>■ Important Dates<br>- Registration deadline: May 8 (online)<br>- Project summary due: May 22<br>- Fair day: June 6 at City Convention Hall<br>■ Categories<br>- Life Science, Physical Science, Engineering, Environmental Science<br>- Each team may enter only one category.<br>■ Awards<br>- First place in each category: &#36;500 and a summer lab internship<br>- Teams sharing a prize will divide the money equally.<br>■ Notes<br>- Projects involving live animals must be approved by a teacher in advance.<br>- Display boards will be provided; electricity is available only on request.</div></div><div class='ch long'><span>① 한 팀은 최대 4명까지 구성할 수 있다.</span><span>② 프로젝트 요약은 등록 마감일과 같은 날 제출한다.</span><span>③ 한 팀이 두 개 부문에 참가할 수 있다.</span><span>④ 부문별 1등에게는 상금과 여름 실험실 인턴십이 주어진다.</span><span>⑤ 전기는 모든 참가자에게 기본으로 제공된다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④ 부문별 1등에게는 상금과 여름 실험실 인턴십이 주어진다.", "sol": "각 부문 1등은 500달러와 여름 실험실 인턴십을 받는다. 팀은 최대 3명, 요약 제출은 5월 22일(등록 마감 5월 8일), 한 부문만 참가, 전기는 요청할 때만 제공된다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.45, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_29": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 밑줄 친 부분 중, 어법상 틀린 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Many people believe that goldfish have a memory of only three seconds. However, this idea is not true. Scientists have found that goldfish can remember things for months. In one experiment, researchers ①<u>trained</u> goldfish to push a lever to get food. The fish learned this quickly and ②<u>remembered</u> it even after a long break. Other studies showed that goldfish can recognize the people ③<u>who</u> feed them. These findings show that goldfish are much ④<u>smarter</u> than we thought. So the next time you see a goldfish, remember that it ⑤<u>have</u> a better memory than you might expect.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "5", "anst": "⑤", "sol": "주어 it이 3인칭 단수이므로 have가 아니라 has가 되어야 한다(수 일치).", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.181, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 밑줄 친 부분 중, 어법상 틀린 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Most of us assume that boredom is simply a waste of time, something ①<u>to avoid</u> at all costs with a phone or a television. Psychologists, however, have begun to see it differently. In one study, participants who were first asked to do a dull task, such as copying numbers from a phone book, later came up with more creative ideas than those ②<u>who</u> had started immediately with the creative task. The researchers suggest that boredom pushes the mind ③<u>to wander</u>, and this wandering allows unexpected connections to form. In this sense, being bored is not the same as ④<u>doing</u> nothing. It may be the quiet stage ⑤<u>which</u> the mind prepares its next good idea.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "5", "anst": "⑤", "sol": "뒤에 완전한 문장(the mind prepares its next good idea)이 오므로 관계대명사 which가 아니라 관계부사 where(또는 in which)가 와야 한다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.296, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 밑줄 친 부분 중, 어법상 틀린 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>The architects of medieval cathedrals worked without the mathematical tools that modern engineers take for granted. They could not calculate the forces ①<u>acting</u> on a stone arch, nor could they test a design with a computer model. What they had instead was accumulated experience: rules of thumb passed from master to apprentice, many of ②<u>which</u> were learned from structures that had collapsed. Seen in this light, the cathedrals that still stand are the survivors of a long and costly process of trial and error. Yet it would be wrong to conclude that the builders were merely lucky. The proportions they used ③<u>were</u> remarkably consistent across Europe, suggesting a shared body of knowledge that was refined over generations. Modern analyses have shown that many of these traditional proportions ④<u>keeps</u> the forces within the stone safely balanced, ⑤<u>which</u> explains why buildings designed by intuition have outlasted some designed by calculation.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④", "sol": "that절의 주어는 many of these traditional proportions(복수)이므로 keeps가 아니라 keep이 되어야 한다. ① forces를 꾸미는 현재분사, ② 앞 명사 rules를 받는 관계대명사(many of which), ③ 주어 The proportions에 맞는 복수 동사, ⑤ 앞 절 전체를 받는 계속적 용법 which.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.45, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_30": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 밑줄 친 부분 중, 문맥상 낱말의 쓰임이 적절하지 않은 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Walking is one of the ①<u>easiest</u> ways to stay healthy. You do not need any special equipment, and you can do it almost anywhere. Doctors say that walking for 30 minutes a day can ②<u>lower</u> the risk of heart disease. It also helps people ③<u>reduce</u> stress and sleep better. Because walking is gentle on the body, it is ④<u>dangerous</u> for people of all ages, including older adults. If you want to start exercising, walking is a ⑤<u>perfect</u> first step.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④", "sol": "걷기는 몸에 무리가 없어 모든 연령에 안전하다는 흐름이므로 dangerous가 아니라 safe가 되어야 한다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.231, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 밑줄 친 부분 중, 문맥상 낱말의 쓰임이 적절하지 않은 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>In many sports, home teams win more often than visiting teams. This \"home advantage\" is usually ①<u>explained</u> by the support of loud local fans. However, research suggests that the crowd may matter in a less ②<u>obvious</u> way. Studies of soccer matches have found that referees tend to give fewer fouls against the home team, especially in close games. Referees are not deliberately ③<u>unfair</u>; rather, the noise of thousands of fans seems to influence their quick judgments without their awareness. Interestingly, during the period when matches were played in empty stadiums, the home advantage ④<u>increased</u> noticeably. This natural experiment ⑤<u>supports</u> the idea that the crowd affects the outcome mainly through the referee.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④", "sol": "관중이 심판을 통해 영향을 준다면 관중이 없을 때 홈 이점은 줄어들어야 한다. 따라서 increased가 아니라 decreased가 맞다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.378, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 밑줄 친 부분 중, 문맥상 낱말의 쓰임이 적절하지 않은 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>It is tempting to think that the most reliable experts are those who express the greatest certainty. A forecaster who speaks in confident absolutes seems to ①<u>possess</u> knowledge that more hesitant colleagues lack. Long-term studies of political and economic predictions, however, point in the opposite direction. Experts who organized their thinking around a single big idea and applied it ②<u>confidently</u> to every question performed worse, on average, than those who drew on many small ideas and adjusted their views as new evidence arrived. The second group's habit of qualifying their claims, often mistaken for ③<u>weakness</u>, turned out to reflect a more accurate sense of what could and could not be known. Ironically, the media tend to ④<u>reward</u> the first type, since bold predictions make better headlines. The result is a public conversation in which the voices heard most often are, by this measure, among the most ⑤<u>trustworthy</u>.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "5", "anst": "⑤", "sol": "확신에 찬 전문가의 예측이 덜 정확한데 언론이 그들의 목소리를 더 자주 들려준다는 흐름이므로, 가장 자주 들리는 목소리는 가장 덜 믿을 만한(least trustworthy / unreliable) 쪽이어야 한다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.533, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_31": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>People often say that teenagers are lazy, or that older people do not like new technology. But these ideas are usually too simple. If you look at a large group of teenagers, you will find some who are lazy, some who work very hard, and many who are somewhere in between. The same is true for older people and technology. Some love their smartphones, and others never use them. In almost every group, there is great <span class='blank'></span> among its members.</p></div><div class='ch'><span>① difference</span><span>② agreement</span><span>③ pressure</span><span>④ similarity</span><span>⑤ competition</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "1", "anst": "① difference", "sol": "어떤 집단이든 게으른 사람, 열심인 사람, 그 중간이 섞여 있다는 내용이므로 구성원 사이의 큰 차이(difference)가 있다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.386, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>When a new medicine is tested, researchers report the average effect: patients' blood pressure dropped by ten points, for example. It is easy to imagine that every patient experienced something close to that number. In reality, the average often hides a wide range of responses. Some patients may have improved dramatically, others not at all, and a few may even have gotten worse. Doctors are increasingly aware that treating the average patient can mean treating no one in particular. This is why modern medicine pays more attention to the <span class='blank'></span> behind the average, trying to identify which kinds of patients benefit most from a treatment and which do not.</p></div><div class='ch'><span>① consistency</span><span>② diversity</span><span>③ accuracy</span><span>④ tradition</span><span>⑤ simplicity</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "② diversity", "sol": "평균 뒤에는 크게 나아진 환자, 효과가 없는 환자, 나빠진 환자가 섞여 있으므로 평균 뒤의 다양성(diversity)에 주목한다는 흐름이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.632, "d": "hi"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Language textbooks present grammar as a fixed system: this is the past tense, this is how questions are formed, and deviations are errors. Linguists who record how people actually speak find something far less tidy. Within a single city, the pronunciation of the same vowel can shift from one neighborhood to the next; within a single family, grandparents and grandchildren may disagree about whether a sentence sounds natural. Such differences are not signs that speakers have failed to learn the rules. They are the raw material from which languages change over time, as one variant gradually spreads and another fades away. A language without this inner <span class='blank'></span> would be a language frozen in place, unable to adapt to the new needs of the people who use it.</p></div><div class='ch'><span>① logic</span><span>② stability</span><span>③ diversity</span><span>④ authority</span><span>⑤ symmetry</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③ diversity", "sol": "한 도시·한 가족 안에서도 발음과 문법 판단이 다르고, 그 차이가 언어 변화의 재료가 된다. 이런 내부 다양성(diversity)이 없으면 언어가 굳어 버린다. stability(안정)는 반대 방향이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.857, "d": "hi"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_32": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Good athletes do not just practice; they also watch videos of their past games. By doing this, they can see which moves worked well and which did not. For example, a soccer player may notice that she often loses the ball when she turns to the left. Then she can practice that move more. In this way, one important habit of top athletes is <span class='blank'></span>.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① playing as many games as possible</span><span>② copying the moves of famous players</span><span>③ looking back at their past performances</span><span>④ listening carefully to their fans</span><span>⑤ resting well before an important game</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③ looking back at their past performances", "sol": "지난 경기 영상을 보며 잘된 동작과 안 된 동작을 찾는다는 내용이므로 과거 경기를 되돌아보는 습관이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.239, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Experienced teachers often say that their most valuable lessons came not from training courses but from their own classrooms. After a lesson, the best of them take a few minutes to ask what happened: Which explanation made students' faces light up? At what point did attention drift away? Which question produced a long silence? Over time, these small reflections add up to a detailed map of what works with real students. A teacher who skips this step may repeat the same lesson for twenty years, while one who keeps it can improve with every class. In this sense, the key to growing as a teacher is the habit of <span class='blank'></span>.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① preparing lessons further in advance</span><span>② reviewing what actually happened in each lesson</span><span>③ attending as many training courses as possible</span><span>④ following the methods of experienced colleagues</span><span>⑤ asking students to evaluate the teacher at the end of the year</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "② reviewing what actually happened in each lesson", "sol": "수업 뒤 무엇이 통했고 어디서 집중이 흐트러졌는지 돌아보는 습관이 성장의 핵심이라는 내용이다. ③은 글이 연수보다 교실 경험을 강조하므로 반대이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.392, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Organizations that handle emergencies, from hospitals to airlines, have learned that disasters are rarely caused by a single dramatic mistake. More often, they result from small weaknesses that line up at the wrong moment. For this reason, many of these organizations hold regular reviews not only after failures but also after near misses and even after operations that went smoothly. A surgical team might ask why a routine procedure took longer than planned, or why a nurse noticed a problem that the checklist had missed. What makes such reviews effective is that their purpose is <span class='blank'></span>. Once staff believe that honest reports will be used to punish individuals, they stop reporting the small problems, and the organization loses exactly the information it needs to prevent the next disaster.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① to identify the person responsible for each error</span><span>② to reduce the time spent on routine procedures</span><span>③ to replace human judgment with detailed checklists</span><span>④ to understand the system rather than to assign blame</span><span>⑤ to celebrate the success of smooth operations</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④ to understand the system rather than to assign blame", "sol": "정직한 보고가 개인 처벌로 쓰이면 보고가 끊긴다고 했으므로, 검토의 목적은 책임자를 찾는 것이 아니라 체계를 이해하는 것이다. ①은 정반대의 매력적인 오답이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.55, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_33": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Weather apps are very useful, but they are not always right. They use data about wind, temperature, and clouds to guess what will happen. This works well for the next few hours. But the weather can change because of many small things, like a sudden wind from the sea. Because of this, a forecast for next week is often wrong. No matter how good the computers become, the weather will always include <span class='blank'></span>.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① some things that we can predict exactly</span><span>② some surprises that data cannot catch</span><span>③ only a few simple patterns</span><span>④ the same changes every year</span><span>⑤ clear rules that never change</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "② some surprises that data cannot catch", "sol": "작은 요인으로 날씨가 바뀌어 일주일 뒤 예보는 자주 틀리므로, 날씨에는 데이터로 잡을 수 없는 놀라움이 늘 있다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.406, "d": "mid"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Translation software has improved enormously. For straightforward texts such as instruction manuals or weather reports, it now produces results that are nearly as good as a human translator's. The trouble begins with texts that depend on context the words themselves do not contain. A joke that relies on a local custom, a poem that plays on the sound of a word, or a letter whose tone shifts between politeness and irritation can all be turned into grammatically correct sentences that completely miss the point. More training data helps, but only up to a point, because the meaning of such texts is shaped by <span class='blank'></span>.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① rules that can be written down in a dictionary</span><span>② patterns that appear in almost every language</span><span>③ the number of words used in each sentence</span><span>④ shared experiences that never appear on the page</span><span>⑤ the speed at which the software processes them</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④ shared experiences that never appear on the page", "sol": "농담·시·편지의 의미는 글자에 없는 맥락에 달려 있어 데이터를 늘려도 한계가 있다. 즉 의미는 지면에 나타나지 않는 공유된 경험이 만든다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.664, "d": "hi"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Economic models have become extraordinarily sophisticated, incorporating millions of data points and tested against decades of history. Yet their record in predicting turning points, such as the start of a recession, remains poor. Part of the reason lies in the nature of the system being modeled. Unlike the planets, whose movements are indifferent to what astronomers say about them, markets are made of people who read forecasts and change their behavior in response. A widely publicized prediction that prices will fall can lead investors to sell early, bringing about the very fall it predicted, or prompt governments to intervene, preventing it altogether. Every successful model thus alters the conditions under which it was built. Economists, in other words, are trying to map a territory that <span class='blank'></span>.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① follows the same laws as the movements of the planets</span><span>② can be fully described once enough data is collected</span><span>③ stays unchanged regardless of what is said about it</span><span>④ shifts in response to the act of mapping it</span><span>⑤ remains hidden from investors and governments</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④ shifts in response to the act of mapping it", "sol": "시장은 예측을 읽고 행동을 바꾸는 사람들로 이루어져 있어 예측 자체가 조건을 바꾼다. 따라서 지도를 그리는 행위에 반응해 변하는 영토이다. ①·③은 행성과 같다는 뜻이라 글과 반대이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.899, "d": "hi"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_34": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Many popular songs today are made by big music companies. But listeners do not only listen to these songs. They also change them. Some people write new words to a famous song to make it funny. Others sing it in their own language or play it on traditional instruments. Children even turn popular songs into playground games. In this way, songs made by companies <span class='blank'></span>.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① are quickly forgotten by most listeners</span><span>② become part of people's own everyday culture</span><span>③ are protected from any changes</span><span>④ lose their value as soon as they are released</span><span>⑤ are played only on the radio</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "② become part of people's own everyday culture", "sol": "사람들이 회사가 만든 노래의 가사를 바꾸고 자기 언어·악기로 부르고 놀이로 만든다. 노래가 사람들의 일상 문화가 된다는 흐름이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.345, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Video game companies spend years designing the worlds their players explore, carefully deciding what is possible within them. Yet some of the most lasting creations in gaming history were never planned by any company. Players have built cities that took months to complete, invented new rules that turned a racing game into a game of hide-and-seek, and written long stories that take place among the characters of their favorite games. Some of these player inventions became so popular that companies later adopted them as official features. Such cases suggest that a game is not finished when it is released; it continues to develop <span class='blank'></span>.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① as companies release regular updates and new versions</span><span>② as players reshape it according to their own imaginations</span><span>③ only when the designers listen to professional critics</span><span>④ until it is replaced by a newer and more popular game</span><span>⑤ as long as players follow the rules set by its designers</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "② as players reshape it according to their own imaginations", "sol": "플레이어가 도시를 짓고 새 규칙을 만들고 이야기를 써서 회사가 계획하지 않은 것을 창조했다. 게임은 출시 뒤에도 플레이어의 상상에 따라 다시 빚어지며 발전한다. ①은 회사 쪽 이야기라 글의 초점과 다르다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.565, "d": "mid"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Museums were long imagined as one-way channels: experts selected objects, wrote the labels, and visitors absorbed the meanings that had been prepared for them. Studies of how people actually move through galleries tell a different story. Families invent games around the exhibits, asking children to find every animal in a room of paintings; teenagers photograph themselves imitating the poses of ancient statues and share the images with captions that have nothing to do with art history; elderly visitors linger in front of an ordinary kitchen tool because it resembles one their mother used. None of these responses is what the curator intended, yet each attaches the object to a life outside the museum walls. In this sense, the meaning of a collection is completed only when <span class='blank'></span>.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① experts agree on how each object should be interpreted</span><span>② visitors learn the historical facts written on the labels</span><span>③ museums prevent the public from taking photographs</span><span>④ visitors fold the objects into their own experiences</span><span>⑤ curators design exhibitions for specific age groups</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④ visitors fold the objects into their own experiences", "sol": "가족·십대·노인 관람객이 큐레이터의 의도와 다르게 전시물을 자기 삶과 연결한다. 따라서 소장품의 의미는 관람객이 그것을 자신의 경험 속에 끌어들일 때 완성된다. ②는 전문가가 준비한 의미를 그대로 받아들이는 옛 관점이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.772, "d": "hi"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_35": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글에서 전체 흐름과 관계 없는 문장은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Cats sleep a lot, usually 12 to 16 hours a day. ①This is because their bodies are made for short bursts of hunting, which use a lot of energy. ②In the wild, cats need to save energy between hunts by resting. ③The price of cat food has risen sharply in many countries over the past few years. ④Even pet cats that never hunt still follow this natural pattern. ⑤So if your cat sleeps most of the day, there is usually nothing to worry about.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③", "sol": "고양이가 오래 자는 이유(사냥에 쓸 에너지 저장)에 관한 글인데, ③은 고양이 사료 가격 이야기라 흐름과 관계없다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.194, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글에서 전체 흐름과 관계 없는 문장은?</p><div class='psg'><p>On hot summer days, a city street lined with trees can be several degrees cooler than a street without them. ①Trees provide shade that keeps sunlight from heating roads and buildings, which would otherwise store heat and release it at night. ②Much of the paper we use every day is made from fast-growing trees planted specifically for that purpose. ③Trees also release water vapor through their leaves, and this process cools the surrounding air in much the same way that sweating cools our skin. ④For these reasons, many cities have begun planting trees as a low-cost way to reduce the health risks of extreme heat. ⑤Some have even set targets to cover a certain percentage of their land with tree canopy within the next decade.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "②", "sol": "도시의 나무가 그늘과 수증기 방출로 거리를 시원하게 한다는 글인데, ②는 종이 원료로 쓰이는 나무 이야기라 관계없다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.317, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글에서 전체 흐름과 관계 없는 문장은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Children who grow up speaking two languages must constantly manage which language to use, with whom, and when. ①Some researchers have argued that this daily practice in switching and suppressing strengthens the mental skills known as executive functions, such as controlling attention and shifting between tasks. ②In early studies, bilingual children outperformed monolingual peers on tasks that required ignoring misleading information, like naming the color of a word printed in a conflicting color. ③More recent and larger studies, however, have often failed to find such advantages, leading to a lively debate about whether the earlier results reflected differences in income or education rather than language. ④People who begin learning a second language after adolescence rarely lose their foreign accent completely, even after decades of practice. ⑤The question remains open, but the debate itself has made researchers more careful about how they define bilingualism and how they compare groups.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④", "sol": "이중 언어 사용이 실행 기능(주의 조절 등)을 강화하는지에 대한 연구와 논쟁의 글인데, ④는 늦게 배운 언어의 억양 이야기라 같은 언어 주제여도 흐름과 관계없다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.455, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_36": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Many people think we yawn because we are tired or bored.</p><p>(A) In one study, people yawned much less often when they held a cold pack against their forehead.</p><p>(B) This suggests that a yawn works a bit like a fan for the brain. So the next time you yawn, your brain may simply be a little too warm.</p><p>(C) But scientists have another idea. They think yawning may help cool down the brain by bringing in cool air.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① (A) - (C) - (B)</span><span>② (B) - (A) - (C)</span><span>③ (B) - (C) - (A)</span><span>④ (C) - (A) - (B)</span><span>⑤ (C) - (B) - (A)</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④ (C) - (A) - (B)", "sol": "피곤해서 하품한다는 통념 → (C) 다른 생각: 뇌를 식힌다 → (A) 차가운 팩 실험 → (B) 이것이 보여 주는 것(뇌의 선풍기).", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.245, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>When a honeybee finds a rich patch of flowers, it returns to the hive carrying valuable information.</p><p>(A) The direction of the straight middle part of this dance tells the other bees which way to fly. If the bee moves straight up the honeycomb, for example, the flowers lie in the direction of the sun.</p><p>(B) To share it, the bee performs what scientists call a \"waggle dance,\" moving in a figure-eight pattern while shaking its body from side to side.</p><p>(C) The length of that straight run, meanwhile, signals distance: the longer the run, the farther away the flowers are. With these two pieces of information, the other bees can fly directly to food they have never seen.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① (A) - (C) - (B)</span><span>② (B) - (A) - (C)</span><span>③ (B) - (C) - (A)</span><span>④ (C) - (A) - (B)</span><span>⑤ (C) - (B) - (A)</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "② (B) - (A) - (C)", "sol": "정보를 가지고 돌아온다 → (B) 그것(it=정보)을 나누려고 8자 춤을 춘다 → (A) 이 춤(this dance)의 가운데 직선 부분이 방향을 알린다 → (C) 그 직선(that straight run)의 길이는 한편(meanwhile) 거리를 알린다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.401, "d": "mid"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>For centuries, doctors regarded the placebo effect as a nuisance — a source of noise that made it harder to tell whether a drug really worked.</p><p>(A) This insight has practical consequences. Some doctors now pay close attention to how they explain a treatment, knowing that their words may strengthen or weaken its effect, and a few studies have even reported benefits from placebos given openly, with patients told exactly what they were taking.</p><p>(B) Such patterns suggested that the effect was produced by the patient's expectations, shaped by every detail of the treatment. If expectations alone could relieve pain, then the ritual of care was itself part of the medicine.</p><p>(C) That view began to change when researchers noticed that the size of the effect was not random. Placebo pills worked better when patients were told they were expensive, and placebo injections often worked better than placebo pills.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① (A) - (C) - (B)</span><span>② (B) - (A) - (C)</span><span>③ (B) - (C) - (A)</span><span>④ (C) - (A) - (B)</span><span>⑤ (C) - (B) - (A)</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "5", "anst": "⑤ (C) - (B) - (A)", "sol": "위약 효과를 방해물로 보던 관점 → (C) 그 관점(That view)이 바뀐 계기: 효과 크기의 규칙성 → (B) 그런 규칙성(Such patterns)은 기대가 효과를 만든다는 뜻 → (A) 이 통찰(This insight)의 실제 결과.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.562, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_37": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Sea otters have a special way of sleeping.</p><p>(A) They often sleep while floating on their backs in the water. But there is a danger: the water might carry them far away while they are asleep.</p><p>(B) Sometimes, sea otters also hold hands with each other while they sleep. This way, the whole group stays together.</p><p>(C) To solve this problem, they wrap themselves in long seaweed that grows up from the sea floor. The seaweed works like an anchor.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① (A) - (C) - (B)</span><span>② (B) - (A) - (C)</span><span>③ (B) - (C) - (A)</span><span>④ (C) - (A) - (B)</span><span>⑤ (C) - (B) - (A)</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "1", "anst": "① (A) - (C) - (B)", "sol": "특별한 잠자는 법 → (A) 등을 대고 떠서 자는데 떠내려갈 위험 → (C) 이 문제(this problem)를 해조류로 해결 → (B) 또한(also) 서로 손을 잡는다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.256, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>For more than a century, scientists believed they knew why ice is slippery.</p><p>(A) Recent research points instead to the surface itself: the molecules at the very top of the ice are only loosely bound and behave almost like a liquid, even without any pressure. It is this naturally slippery layer, not the skater, that makes ice so hard to stand on.</p><p>(B) According to the traditional explanation, the pressure of a skate blade melts a thin layer of ice, and the skater glides on the resulting water.</p><p>(C) Calculations later showed, however, that a person's weight produces far too little pressure to melt ice at ordinary winter temperatures. Something else had to be going on.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① (A) - (C) - (B)</span><span>② (B) - (A) - (C)</span><span>③ (B) - (C) - (A)</span><span>④ (C) - (A) - (B)</span><span>⑤ (C) - (B) - (A)</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③ (B) - (C) - (A)", "sol": "얼음이 왜 미끄러운지 안다고 믿었다 → (B) 전통적 설명: 날의 압력이 얼음을 녹인다 → (C) 그러나 압력이 너무 작다는 계산, 다른 이유가 있어야 한다 → (A) 최근 연구는 대신(instead) 표면 자체를 가리킨다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.419, "d": "mid"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>A banknote is, physically, a nearly worthless piece of paper, yet people will work for weeks to obtain a stack of them.</p><p>(A) This is why money can lose its value with alarming speed. When people begin to doubt that others will continue to accept a currency, they rush to spend it before it becomes worthless, and their rush makes the doubt come true.</p><p>(B) Its value, therefore, cannot lie in the object itself. It lies in a shared belief: each person accepts the note because he or she expects that others will accept it in turn.</p><p>(C) Such collapses explain why governments that understand money protect it less with gold in a vault than with consistent policies. What they are really defending is not a stock of metal but a network of expectations.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① (A) - (C) - (B)</span><span>② (B) - (A) - (C)</span><span>③ (B) - (C) - (A)</span><span>④ (C) - (A) - (B)</span><span>⑤ (C) - (B) - (A)</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "② (B) - (A) - (C)", "sol": "지폐는 물리적으로 거의 가치가 없다 → (B) 그러니 가치는 공유된 믿음에 있다 → (A) 그래서(This is why) 믿음이 흔들리면 화폐 가치가 급락한다 → (C) 그런 붕괴(Such collapses) 때문에 정부는 일관된 정책으로 기대의 망을 지킨다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.586, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_38": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>글의 흐름으로 보아, 주어진 문장이 들어가기에 가장 적절한 곳은?</p><div class='given'>These features make flying impossible, but they are perfect for life in the water.</div><div class='psg'><p>Most birds can fly, and they use their wings to travel long distances. ( ① ) Penguins, however, cannot fly at all. ( ② ) Their wings are short and stiff, and their bodies are heavy and solid. ( ③ ) Instead, they use their wings like flippers to move through the water. ( ④ ) Some penguins can swim faster than 30 kilometers per hour. ( ⑤ ) So, although penguins cannot fly in the sky, you could say that they \"fly\" through the sea.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③", "sol": "주어진 문장의 These features(짧고 뻣뻣한 날개, 무거운 몸)는 ③ 앞 문장을 가리키고, 뒤의 Instead(대신 지느러미처럼 쓴다)와 이어진다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.311, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>글의 흐름으로 보아, 주어진 문장이 들어가기에 가장 적절한 곳은?</p><div class='given'>This protection, however, does not come for free.</div><div class='psg'><p>Many plants cannot run away from the animals that eat them, so they defend themselves with chemistry. ( ① ) Tomatoes, potatoes, and tobacco, for example, produce bitter compounds that make their leaves unpleasant or even poisonous to insects. ( ② ) Producing these chemicals requires energy and nutrients that could otherwise be used for growth and reproduction. ( ③ ) For this reason, many plants keep their defenses switched off until they are actually attacked. ( ④ ) When a caterpillar begins chewing on a leaf, the damaged cells release signals that spread through the plant and trigger the production of defensive chemicals. ( ⑤ ) Some plants even release signals into the air that warn their neighbors to prepare their own defenses.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "②", "sol": "화학 방어(This protection)가 공짜가 아니라는 문장은 쓴 화학 물질의 예 다음, 그 비용(에너지와 영양분이 든다)을 설명하는 문장 앞인 ②에 들어가야 한다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.508, "d": "mid"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>글의 흐름으로 보아, 주어진 문장이 들어가기에 가장 적절한 곳은?</p><div class='given'>What had changed was not the human brain but the environment in which it operated.</div><div class='psg'><p>During the twentieth century, average scores on intelligence tests rose by about three points per decade in many countries, a trend known as the Flynn effect. ( ① ) The rise was far too fast to be explained by genetics, since the genes of a population cannot change meaningfully in a few generations. ( ② ) Nor could it be explained simply by people becoming familiar with tests, because some of the largest gains appeared on puzzles unlike anything taught in school. ( ③ ) Over the same period, schooling lengthened, nutrition improved, and jobs increasingly demanded abstract reasoning — classifying, forming hypotheses, and thinking about situations that did not actually exist. ( ④ ) People who grew up in such a world practiced, every day, the very skills that the tests measured. ( ⑤ ) Interestingly, in some countries the scores have recently stopped rising, a reminder that the effect depends on conditions that can change again.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③", "sol": "유전과 시험 익숙함이라는 두 설명을 부정한 뒤, 바뀐 것은 뇌가 아니라 환경이라는 문장이 와야 그 환경의 구체적 변화(학교, 영양, 일)가 이어진다. ②에 넣으면 Nor로 시작하는 문장의 연결이 끊긴다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.7, "d": "hi"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_39": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>글의 흐름으로 보아, 주어진 문장이 들어가기에 가장 적절한 곳은?</p><div class='given'>However, too much sugar can be bad for your health.</div><div class='psg'><p>Sugar gives our bodies quick energy. ( ① ) That is why many athletes eat a little sugar before a game. ( ② ) It also makes food taste sweet and delicious. ( ③ ) Eating a lot of sugar can cause problems like tooth decay and weight gain. ( ④ ) So doctors say we should check how much sugar is in the food we eat. ( ⑤ ) Choosing fruit instead of candy is a good way to start.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③", "sol": "설탕의 좋은 점(에너지, 맛) 다음, 많이 먹으면 생기는 문제 앞인 ③에 However가 이끄는 문장이 들어가야 한다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.309, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>글의 흐름으로 보아, 주어진 문장이 들어가기에 가장 적절한 곳은?</p><div class='given'>Yet the same quality that makes them so useful also makes them dangerous.</div><div class='psg'><p>Plastics are among the most useful materials ever invented. ( ① ) They are light, cheap, and, above all, durable: a plastic bottle can survive drops, heat, and years on a shelf without breaking down. ( ② ) Because plastics resist decay, most of the plastic ever produced still exists somewhere, much of it in landfills and oceans. ( ③ ) Over time, sunlight and waves break large pieces into tiny fragments called microplastics. ( ④ ) These particles have been found in deep-sea creatures, in drinking water, and even in human blood. ( ⑤ ) Scientists are now developing plastics designed to break down safely, but replacing a material so deeply built into modern life will take decades.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "②", "sol": "쓸모 있게 만드는 바로 그 성질(내구성)이 위험하게도 만든다는 문장은 내구성 설명 다음, 썩지 않아 계속 남는다는 문장 앞인 ②에 들어간다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.505, "d": "mid"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>글의 흐름으로 보아, 주어진 문장이 들어가기에 가장 적절한 곳은?</p><div class='given'>What had looked like waste, in other words, turned out to be insurance.</div><div class='psg'><p>For decades, businesses have worked to eliminate idle inventory. ( ① ) Goods sitting in a warehouse represent money that is not earning anything, so companies learned to order parts only when they needed them, keeping their shelves nearly empty. ( ② ) The approach saved enormous sums and became a standard of good management. ( ③ ) Its weakness was revealed when a single disruption, such as a factory fire or a blocked shipping route, left entire industries without parts for months. ( ④ ) Companies that had kept a few weeks of extra stock, long criticized as inefficient, were able to continue production while their competitors shut down. ( ⑤ ) Engineers have long recognized a similar principle: a bridge designed to carry exactly its expected load is poorly designed, because real loads are never exactly as expected.</p></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "5", "anst": "⑤", "sol": "낭비처럼 보였던 것이 보험이었다는 문장은 비효율적이라 비판받던 여분 재고 덕에 생산을 이어 갔다는 ④ 뒤 문장 다음에 와야 하고, 그 뒤 a similar principle(비슷한 원리)이 이 문장을 받는다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.695, "d": "hi"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_40": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 내용을 한 문장으로 요약하고자 한다. 빈칸 (A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>When you do something for the first time, like riding a bike, you have to think about every move. Your brain works very hard. But after you practice many times, you can ride without thinking about it at all. Scientists say that repeated actions move to a part of the brain that controls habits. This part works automatically and uses much less energy. Because of this, the thinking part of your brain is free to focus on other things.</p><p class='sum'>Repeating an action makes it more <span class='blank'>(A)</span>, which <span class='blank'>(B)</span> the brain's energy.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① difficult ······· wastes</span><span>② exciting ······· increases</span><span>③ automatic ······· saves</span><span>④ automatic ······· wastes</span><span>⑤ difficult ······· saves</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③ automatic ······· saves", "sol": "반복하면 행동이 자동으로 이루어지고(automatic) 에너지를 훨씬 덜 쓰므로 뇌의 에너지를 아낀다(saves).", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.151, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 내용을 한 문장으로 요약하고자 한다. 빈칸 (A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>Students who expect to teach material to others often learn it better than those who study only for a test, a pattern sometimes called the protégé effect. When people prepare to explain an idea, they cannot simply recognize that a page looks familiar. They have to organize the information, put it into their own words, and predict the questions a listener might ask. In the process, they often discover that parts they thought they understood are actually unclear to them. Fixing these weak spots before the explanation leads to a deeper and longer-lasting understanding than rereading the same notes several times.</p><p class='sum'>Teaching others what we have learned <span class='blank'>(A)</span> our understanding because it forces us to <span class='blank'>(B)</span> the gaps in our knowledge.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① weakens ······· hide</span><span>② deepens ······· notice</span><span>③ deepens ······· ignore</span><span>④ tests ······· create</span><span>⑤ replaces ······· notice</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "② deepens ······· notice", "sol": "가르칠 준비를 하며 이해했다고 생각한 부분의 빈틈을 발견하고(notice) 고치면서 이해가 깊어진다(deepens).", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.247, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>다음 글의 내용을 한 문장으로 요약하고자 한다. 빈칸 (A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='psg'><p>In a well-known experiment in a grocery store, researchers set up a tasting table that offered either six or twenty-four varieties of jam. The larger display attracted noticeably more shoppers, who seemed delighted by the abundance. Yet when it came to actually buying, the pattern reversed: shoppers who had seen only six jams were far more likely to purchase one. Later studies suggested a reason. Each additional option increases the effort required to compare alternatives, and it also raises the fear of having chosen wrongly, since the perfect jam might have been just one jar away. Faced with too many possibilities, people often postpone the decision entirely, and those who do choose tend to feel less satisfied with what they picked.</p><p class='sum'>Although a wide range of options seems <span class='blank'>(A)</span> to consumers, it can actually <span class='blank'>(B)</span> their willingness to decide and their satisfaction afterward.</p></div><div class='ch long'><span>① appealing ······· reduce</span><span>② confusing ······· increase</span><span>③ appealing ······· increase</span><span>④ unnecessary ······· reduce</span><span>⑤ confusing ······· reveal</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "1", "anst": "① appealing ······· reduce", "sol": "선택지가 많은 진열대가 더 많은 손님을 끌었으므로 매력적으로 보이지만(appealing), 실제로는 구매 결정과 만족을 줄인다(reduce). (A)에 confusing을 넣으면 손님이 즐거워했다는 내용과 맞지 않는다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.45, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_41": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>[41~42] 다음 글을 읽고, 물음에 답하시오.</p><div class='psg'><p>People laugh about thirty times more often when they are with others than when they are alone. This shows that laughter is mainly a <u>(a) social</u> behavior. When we laugh together, we send a simple message: \"I am friendly, and I feel safe with you.\" This message helps people <u>(b) build</u> closer relationships. Scientists have also found that laughing together releases chemicals in the brain that make us feel good. These good feelings make us want to <u>(c) avoid</u> the same people again and again. Interestingly, most of the things we laugh at are not very funny. We usually laugh at ordinary comments, not at clever jokes. This means that laughter is less about humor and more about <u>(d) connection</u>. So, if you want to make new friends, don't worry about being <u>(e) funny</u>. Just share a laugh.</p></div><p class='stem q2'>41. 윗글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='ch long'><span>① Laughter: A Bridge Between People</span><span>② How to Tell a Really Good Joke</span><span>③ Why Some People Rarely Laugh</span><span>④ The Health Risks of Daily Stress</span><span>⑤ Funny Movies Make Us Smarter</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "1", "anst": "① Laughter: A Bridge Between People", "sol": "웃음은 주로 사회적 행동이며 사람들을 더 가깝게 이어 준다는 내용이므로 \"웃음: 사람 사이의 다리\"가 알맞다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.183, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>[41~42] 다음 글을 읽고, 물음에 답하시오.</p><div class='psg'><p>Every human society cooks its food, and some scientists believe that cooking did far more than make meals tasty: it may have <u>(a) shaped</u> the course of our evolution. Raw plants and meat are tough to chew and hard to digest. Chimpanzees, our closest living relatives, spend several hours a day simply chewing. Cooking <u>(b) breaks down</u> tough fibers and makes nutrients easier to absorb, so the same food provides more energy for less effort. According to the anthropologist Richard Wrangham, this extra energy may have allowed our ancestors' brains to <u>(c) grow</u> larger, since the brain is one of the most energy-hungry organs in the body. At the same time, cooked food <u>(d) increased</u> the need for large teeth and powerful jaws, which gradually became smaller over many generations. Cooking changed social life as well. Gathering around a fire to prepare and share food created regular occasions for cooperation and conversation, which may have <u>(e) strengthened</u> the bonds within early human groups.</p></div><p class='stem q2'>41. 윗글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='ch long'><span>① How Fire Helped Humans Survive Cold Winters</span><span>② Cooking: A Key Ingredient in Becoming Human</span><span>③ Why Chimpanzees Spend So Long Eating</span><span>④ Raw Food Diets and Their Health Benefits</span><span>⑤ The Brain: An Organ That Wastes Energy</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "② Cooking: A Key Ingredient in Becoming Human", "sol": "요리가 뇌 성장, 치아·턱의 변화, 사회적 유대까지 인류 진화에 영향을 주었다는 내용이므로 \"요리: 인간이 되는 핵심 재료\"가 알맞다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.299, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>[41~42] 다음 글을 읽고, 물음에 답하시오.</p><div class='psg'><p>We tend to treat forgetting as a failure, a gap where knowledge should be. Yet a growing body of research suggests that forgetting is not simply the passive <u>(a) decay</u> of memory but an active process that the brain carries out on purpose. Neuroscientists have identified mechanisms that weaken the connections between neurons, and some of these mechanisms appear to be especially <u>(b) active</u> during sleep and rest. Why would the brain work to erase what it has stored? One answer is that a memory system is valuable not for how much it retains but for how well it supports <u>(c) decisions</u>. A mind that recorded every detail of every experience would struggle to see the patterns that matter; each new situation would be buried under countless irrelevant particulars. By letting go of specifics, the brain is able to <u>(d) generalize</u>, extracting the gist of past experiences and applying it to new circumstances. Forgetting also allows us to update what we know. If you move to a new home, remembering your old address too vividly would only <u>(e) help</u> you as you try to fill out forms with the new one. From this perspective, the purpose of memory is not to preserve the past but to prepare us for the future, and forgetting is one of its most important tools.</p></div><p class='stem q2'>41. 윗글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='ch long'><span>① The Hidden Costs of Studying Too Late at Night</span><span>② Forgetting on Purpose: How Memory Serves the Future</span><span>③ Sleep: The Best Time to Learn New Facts</span><span>④ Why We Remember Our Childhood Homes</span><span>⑤ Training the Brain to Store More Details</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "② Forgetting on Purpose: How Memory Serves the Future", "sol": "망각은 뇌가 일부러 하는 능동적 과정이며 일반화와 갱신을 도와 미래를 준비하게 한다는 내용이므로 ②가 알맞다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.45, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_42": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>[41~42] 다음 글을 읽고, 물음에 답하시오.</p><div class='psg'><p>People laugh about thirty times more often when they are with others than when they are alone. This shows that laughter is mainly a <u>(a) social</u> behavior. When we laugh together, we send a simple message: \"I am friendly, and I feel safe with you.\" This message helps people <u>(b) build</u> closer relationships. Scientists have also found that laughing together releases chemicals in the brain that make us feel good. These good feelings make us want to <u>(c) avoid</u> the same people again and again. Interestingly, most of the things we laugh at are not very funny. We usually laugh at ordinary comments, not at clever jokes. This means that laughter is less about humor and more about <u>(d) connection</u>. So, if you want to make new friends, don't worry about being <u>(e) funny</u>. Just share a laugh.</p></div><p class='stem q2'>42. 밑줄 친 (a)~(e) 중에서 문맥상 낱말의 쓰임이 적절하지 않은 것은?</p><div class='ch'><span>① (a)</span><span>② (b)</span><span>③ (c)</span><span>④ (d)</span><span>⑤ (e)</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③ (c)", "sol": "함께 웃으면 기분이 좋아져 같은 사람들과 더 시간을 보내고 싶어진다는 흐름이므로 (c) avoid(피하다)가 아니라 meet/see(만나다) 같은 말이 와야 한다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.167, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>[41~42] 다음 글을 읽고, 물음에 답하시오.</p><div class='psg'><p>Every human society cooks its food, and some scientists believe that cooking did far more than make meals tasty: it may have <u>(a) shaped</u> the course of our evolution. Raw plants and meat are tough to chew and hard to digest. Chimpanzees, our closest living relatives, spend several hours a day simply chewing. Cooking <u>(b) breaks down</u> tough fibers and makes nutrients easier to absorb, so the same food provides more energy for less effort. According to the anthropologist Richard Wrangham, this extra energy may have allowed our ancestors' brains to <u>(c) grow</u> larger, since the brain is one of the most energy-hungry organs in the body. At the same time, cooked food <u>(d) increased</u> the need for large teeth and powerful jaws, which gradually became smaller over many generations. Cooking changed social life as well. Gathering around a fire to prepare and share food created regular occasions for cooperation and conversation, which may have <u>(e) strengthened</u> the bonds within early human groups.</p></div><p class='stem q2'>42. 밑줄 친 (a)~(e) 중에서 문맥상 낱말의 쓰임이 적절하지 않은 것은?</p><div class='ch'><span>① (a)</span><span>② (b)</span><span>③ (c)</span><span>④ (d)</span><span>⑤ (e)</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④ (d)", "sol": "익힌 음식은 씹기 쉬워 큰 이와 강한 턱의 필요를 줄였고, 그래서 이와 턱이 점점 작아졌다. 따라서 (d) increased가 아니라 reduced가 맞다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.274, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>[41~42] 다음 글을 읽고, 물음에 답하시오.</p><div class='psg'><p>We tend to treat forgetting as a failure, a gap where knowledge should be. Yet a growing body of research suggests that forgetting is not simply the passive <u>(a) decay</u> of memory but an active process that the brain carries out on purpose. Neuroscientists have identified mechanisms that weaken the connections between neurons, and some of these mechanisms appear to be especially <u>(b) active</u> during sleep and rest. Why would the brain work to erase what it has stored? One answer is that a memory system is valuable not for how much it retains but for how well it supports <u>(c) decisions</u>. A mind that recorded every detail of every experience would struggle to see the patterns that matter; each new situation would be buried under countless irrelevant particulars. By letting go of specifics, the brain is able to <u>(d) generalize</u>, extracting the gist of past experiences and applying it to new circumstances. Forgetting also allows us to update what we know. If you move to a new home, remembering your old address too vividly would only <u>(e) help</u> you as you try to fill out forms with the new one. From this perspective, the purpose of memory is not to preserve the past but to prepare us for the future, and forgetting is one of its most important tools.</p></div><p class='stem q2'>42. 밑줄 친 (a)~(e) 중에서 문맥상 낱말의 쓰임이 적절하지 않은 것은?</p><div class='ch'><span>① (a)</span><span>② (b)</span><span>③ (c)</span><span>④ (d)</span><span>⑤ (e)</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "5", "anst": "⑤ (e)", "sol": "옛 주소를 너무 생생하게 기억하면 새 주소를 쓸 때 방해가 된다는 흐름이므로 (e) help가 아니라 confuse/hinder 같은 말이 와야 한다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.45, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_43": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>[43~45] 다음 글을 읽고, 물음에 답하시오.</p><div class='psg'><p class='lab'>(A)</p><p>Lily loved to draw, but she never showed her pictures to anyone. One day, her teacher, Mr. Brown, announced a school art contest. \"Everyone should try,\" he said with a smile. Lily looked down at her desk. <u>(a) She</u> was too shy to enter.</p><p class='lab'>(B)</p><p>A week later, the winners were announced in the school hall. Lily's name was called for second place. As she walked to the front, Mr. Brown clapped the loudest. Lily thought that <u>(e) she</u> would never hide her drawings again.</p><p class='lab'>(C)</p><p>After class, Mr. Brown found Lily's sketchbook, which <u>(b) she</u> had left on her desk. He opened it and was surprised by the beautiful drawings of birds. The next day, he gave it back to her while <u>(c) she</u> was packing her bag. \"Your birds look like they could fly off the page,\" he said.</p><p class='lab'>(D)</p><p>Those words gave Lily courage. That evening, she chose her best drawing, a blue bird sitting on a branch, and worked on it until late at night. The next morning, she handed it to Mr. Brown with shaking hands, and <u>(d) he</u> promised to enter it in the contest.</p></div><p class='stem q2'>43. 주어진 글 (A)에 이어질 내용을 순서에 맞게 배열한 것으로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='ch long'><span>① (B) - (D) - (C)</span><span>② (C) - (B) - (D)</span><span>③ (C) - (D) - (B)</span><span>④ (D) - (B) - (C)</span><span>⑤ (D) - (C) - (B)</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③ (C) - (D) - (B)", "sol": "(A) 수줍어 대회에 나가지 않음 → (C) 선생님이 두고 간 스케치북을 보고 칭찬 → (D) 그 말(Those words)에 용기를 얻어 그림을 냄 → (B) 일주일 뒤 2등.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.083, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>[43~45] 다음 글을 읽고, 물음에 답하시오.</p><div class='psg'><p class='lab'>(A)</p><p>Every Saturday, Sam visited his grandfather's small watch repair shop. The shop was full of ticking clocks, and Sam loved watching his grandfather work with tiny tools. One morning, a woman came in holding an old pocket watch. \"It belonged to my father,\" she said. \"It stopped years ago, and every shop says it can't be fixed.\" Sam's grandfather turned the watch over gently and told her that <u>(a) he</u> would do his best.</p><p class='lab'>(B)</p><p>For days, the grandfather worked on the watch after the shop closed. The problem was a broken gear so small and old that no replacement could be bought anywhere. Sam watched <u>(b) him</u> try one solution after another, but each attempt failed. On the tenth night, he put down his tools and rubbed his eyes. \"Maybe they were right,\" he said quietly.</p><p class='lab'>(C)</p><p>When the woman returned, the watch was ticking softly on the counter. She held it to her ear, and her eyes filled with tears. \"I can hear my father again,\" she whispered. After she left, Sam's grandfather put a hand on Sam's shoulder and said, \"You saved that watch. Someday this shop will be yours.\" Sam smiled, already imagining the day <u>(e) he</u> would sit at the workbench himself.</p><p class='lab'>(D)</p><p>Sam thought for a moment and then remembered the box of broken watches under the counter. \"Grandpa, what about those?\" Sam asked. His grandfather glanced at the dusty box. At first <u>(c) he</u> looked doubtful, but then he nodded. Together they searched through dozens of old watches until, near midnight, they found a gear of exactly the right size. His grandfather held it up to the light, and for the first time in days, <u>(d) he</u> laughed.</p></div><p class='stem q2'>43. 주어진 글 (A)에 이어질 내용을 순서에 맞게 배열한 것으로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='ch long'><span>① (B) - (D) - (C)</span><span>② (C) - (B) - (D)</span><span>③ (C) - (D) - (B)</span><span>④ (D) - (B) - (C)</span><span>⑤ (D) - (C) - (B)</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "1", "anst": "① (B) - (D) - (C)", "sol": "(A) 고칠 수 없다는 시계를 맡음 → (B) 맞는 톱니를 구할 수 없어 실패 → (D) Sam이 고장 난 시계 상자를 떠올려 톱니를 찾음 → (C) 여자가 돌아와 시계 소리를 들음.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.135, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>[43~45] 다음 글을 읽고, 물음에 답하시오.</p><div class='psg'><p class='lab'>(A)</p><p>When Hana was offered a seat in the city youth orchestra, she assumed it would be in the first violin section, where she had always played. Instead, the conductor, Ms. Ortiz, placed her among the second violins, a section whose parts were mostly accompaniment. Hana accepted politely, but at home she told her mother that <u>(a) she</u> had been punished for something she did not understand.</p><p class='lab'>(B)</p><p>At the concert, Hana played the same notes she had played for weeks, but they no longer felt like the same notes. When the slow movement ended, the hall was silent for a moment before the applause began. Afterward, Ms. Ortiz found her backstage. \"Next season,\" <u>(c) she</u> said, \"I would like you to lead the second violins.\" Hana realized that <u>(d) she</u> was being offered not a consolation but a responsibility, and that <u>(e) she</u> wanted it.</p><p class='lab'>(C)</p><p>\"Neither line is the music,\" Ms. Ortiz said. \"The music is what happens between them.\" After rehearsal, she handed Hana a recording of a famous string quartet. \"Listen to the second violinist,\" she said, \"not the first.\" That night Hana listened four times. By the fourth, <u>(b) she</u> could hear how the second violin shaped every phrase the first violin played, leaning into a chord just before the melody rose and holding back so that the melody could breathe.</p><p class='lab'>(D)</p><p>At the first rehearsals, Hana played her part flawlessly but with little interest, her eyes drifting toward the first violins whenever they had the melody. Ms. Ortiz said nothing about it. Then, a week before the concert, she stopped the orchestra in the middle of a slow movement and asked the second violins to play their line alone. To Hana's surprise, the harmony she had been treating as background sounded thin and uncertain on its own, yet the melody, when the first violins tried it alone, sounded strangely empty too.</p></div><p class='stem q2'>43. 주어진 글 (A)에 이어질 내용을 순서에 맞게 배열한 것으로 가장 적절한 것은?</p><div class='ch long'><span>① (B) - (D) - (C)</span><span>② (C) - (B) - (D)</span><span>③ (C) - (D) - (B)</span><span>④ (D) - (B) - (C)</span><span>⑤ (D) - (C) - (B)</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "5", "anst": "⑤ (D) - (C) - (B)", "sol": "(A) 제2바이올린에 배정되어 실망 → (D) 무심하게 연주하다가 각 파트를 따로 연주해 보는 경험 → (C) \"어느 쪽도 음악이 아니다\"라는 말과 사중주 녹음 → (B) 연주회 뒤 제2바이올린 리더 제안.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["en"], "r": 0.45, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_44": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>[43~45] 다음 글을 읽고, 물음에 답하시오.</p><div class='psg'><p class='lab'>(A)</p><p>Lily loved to draw, but she never showed her pictures to anyone. One day, her teacher, Mr. Brown, announced a school art contest. \"Everyone should try,\" he said with a smile. Lily looked down at her desk. <u>(a) She</u> was too shy to enter.</p><p class='lab'>(B)</p><p>A week later, the winners were announced in the school hall. Lily's name was called for second place. As she walked to the front, Mr. Brown clapped the loudest. Lily thought that <u>(e) she</u> would never hide her drawings again.</p><p class='lab'>(C)</p><p>After class, Mr. Brown found Lily's sketchbook, which <u>(b) she</u> had left on her desk. He opened it and was surprised by the beautiful drawings of birds. The next day, he gave it back to her while <u>(c) she</u> was packing her bag. \"Your birds look like they could fly off the page,\" he said.</p><p class='lab'>(D)</p><p>Those words gave Lily courage. That evening, she chose her best drawing, a blue bird sitting on a branch, and worked on it until late at night. The next morning, she handed it to Mr. Brown with shaking hands, and <u>(d) he</u> promised to enter it in the contest.</p></div><p class='stem q2'>44. 밑줄 친 (a)~(e) 중에서 가리키는 대상이 나머지 넷과 다른 것은?</p><div class='ch'><span>① (a)</span><span>② (b)</span><span>③ (c)</span><span>④ (d)</span><span>⑤ (e)</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "4", "anst": "④ (d)", "sol": "(a)(b)(c)(e)는 Lily, (d)는 Mr. Brown이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.151, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>[43~45] 다음 글을 읽고, 물음에 답하시오.</p><div class='psg'><p class='lab'>(A)</p><p>Every Saturday, Sam visited his grandfather's small watch repair shop. The shop was full of ticking clocks, and Sam loved watching his grandfather work with tiny tools. One morning, a woman came in holding an old pocket watch. \"It belonged to my father,\" she said. \"It stopped years ago, and every shop says it can't be fixed.\" Sam's grandfather turned the watch over gently and told her that <u>(a) he</u> would do his best.</p><p class='lab'>(B)</p><p>For days, the grandfather worked on the watch after the shop closed. The problem was a broken gear so small and old that no replacement could be bought anywhere. Sam watched <u>(b) him</u> try one solution after another, but each attempt failed. On the tenth night, he put down his tools and rubbed his eyes. \"Maybe they were right,\" he said quietly.</p><p class='lab'>(C)</p><p>When the woman returned, the watch was ticking softly on the counter. She held it to her ear, and her eyes filled with tears. \"I can hear my father again,\" she whispered. After she left, Sam's grandfather put a hand on Sam's shoulder and said, \"You saved that watch. Someday this shop will be yours.\" Sam smiled, already imagining the day <u>(e) he</u> would sit at the workbench himself.</p><p class='lab'>(D)</p><p>Sam thought for a moment and then remembered the box of broken watches under the counter. \"Grandpa, what about those?\" Sam asked. His grandfather glanced at the dusty box. At first <u>(c) he</u> looked doubtful, but then he nodded. Together they searched through dozens of old watches until, near midnight, they found a gear of exactly the right size. His grandfather held it up to the light, and for the first time in days, <u>(d) he</u> laughed.</p></div><p class='stem q2'>44. 밑줄 친 (a)~(e) 중에서 가리키는 대상이 나머지 넷과 다른 것은?</p><div class='ch'><span>① (a)</span><span>② (b)</span><span>③ (c)</span><span>④ (d)</span><span>⑤ (e)</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "5", "anst": "⑤ (e)", "sol": "(a)(b)(c)(d)는 할아버지, (e)는 Sam이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.247, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>[43~45] 다음 글을 읽고, 물음에 답하시오.</p><div class='psg'><p class='lab'>(A)</p><p>When Hana was offered a seat in the city youth orchestra, she assumed it would be in the first violin section, where she had always played. Instead, the conductor, Ms. Ortiz, placed her among the second violins, a section whose parts were mostly accompaniment. Hana accepted politely, but at home she told her mother that <u>(a) she</u> had been punished for something she did not understand.</p><p class='lab'>(B)</p><p>At the concert, Hana played the same notes she had played for weeks, but they no longer felt like the same notes. When the slow movement ended, the hall was silent for a moment before the applause began. Afterward, Ms. Ortiz found her backstage. \"Next season,\" <u>(c) she</u> said, \"I would like you to lead the second violins.\" Hana realized that <u>(d) she</u> was being offered not a consolation but a responsibility, and that <u>(e) she</u> wanted it.</p><p class='lab'>(C)</p><p>\"Neither line is the music,\" Ms. Ortiz said. \"The music is what happens between them.\" After rehearsal, she handed Hana a recording of a famous string quartet. \"Listen to the second violinist,\" she said, \"not the first.\" That night Hana listened four times. By the fourth, <u>(b) she</u> could hear how the second violin shaped every phrase the first violin played, leaning into a chord just before the melody rose and holding back so that the melody could breathe.</p><p class='lab'>(D)</p><p>At the first rehearsals, Hana played her part flawlessly but with little interest, her eyes drifting toward the first violins whenever they had the melody. Ms. Ortiz said nothing about it. Then, a week before the concert, she stopped the orchestra in the middle of a slow movement and asked the second violins to play their line alone. To Hana's surprise, the harmony she had been treating as background sounded thin and uncertain on its own, yet the melody, when the first violins tried it alone, sounded strangely empty too.</p></div><p class='stem q2'>44. 밑줄 친 (a)~(e) 중에서 가리키는 대상이 나머지 넷과 다른 것은?</p><div class='ch'><span>① (a)</span><span>② (b)</span><span>③ (c)</span><span>④ (d)</span><span>⑤ (e)</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③ (c)", "sol": "(a)(b)(d)(e)는 Hana, (c)는 Ms. Ortiz이다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": [], "r": 0.45, "d": "mid"}], "en_2026_10월학력평가_45": [{"lv": 1, "q": "<p class='stem'>[43~45] 다음 글을 읽고, 물음에 답하시오.</p><div class='psg'><p class='lab'>(A)</p><p>Lily loved to draw, but she never showed her pictures to anyone. One day, her teacher, Mr. Brown, announced a school art contest. \"Everyone should try,\" he said with a smile. Lily looked down at her desk. <u>(a) She</u> was too shy to enter.</p><p class='lab'>(B)</p><p>A week later, the winners were announced in the school hall. Lily's name was called for second place. As she walked to the front, Mr. Brown clapped the loudest. Lily thought that <u>(e) she</u> would never hide her drawings again.</p><p class='lab'>(C)</p><p>After class, Mr. Brown found Lily's sketchbook, which <u>(b) she</u> had left on her desk. He opened it and was surprised by the beautiful drawings of birds. The next day, he gave it back to her while <u>(c) she</u> was packing her bag. \"Your birds look like they could fly off the page,\" he said.</p><p class='lab'>(D)</p><p>Those words gave Lily courage. That evening, she chose her best drawing, a blue bird sitting on a branch, and worked on it until late at night. The next morning, she handed it to Mr. Brown with shaking hands, and <u>(d) he</u> promised to enter it in the contest.</p></div><p class='stem q2'>45. 윗글에 관한 내용으로 적절하지 않은 것은?</p><div class='ch long'><span>① Mr. Brown은 학교 미술 대회를 알렸다.</span><span>② Lily는 스케치북을 책상에 두고 갔다.</span><span>③ Lily의 스케치북에는 새 그림이 있었다.</span><span>④ Lily는 파란 새 그림을 다음 날 아침에 냈다.</span><span>⑤ Lily는 대회에서 1등을 했다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "5", "anst": "⑤ Lily는 대회에서 1등을 했다.", "sol": "Lily는 2등(second place)을 했다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.083, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 2, "q": "<p class='stem'>[43~45] 다음 글을 읽고, 물음에 답하시오.</p><div class='psg'><p class='lab'>(A)</p><p>Every Saturday, Sam visited his grandfather's small watch repair shop. The shop was full of ticking clocks, and Sam loved watching his grandfather work with tiny tools. One morning, a woman came in holding an old pocket watch. \"It belonged to my father,\" she said. \"It stopped years ago, and every shop says it can't be fixed.\" Sam's grandfather turned the watch over gently and told her that <u>(a) he</u> would do his best.</p><p class='lab'>(B)</p><p>For days, the grandfather worked on the watch after the shop closed. The problem was a broken gear so small and old that no replacement could be bought anywhere. Sam watched <u>(b) him</u> try one solution after another, but each attempt failed. On the tenth night, he put down his tools and rubbed his eyes. \"Maybe they were right,\" he said quietly.</p><p class='lab'>(C)</p><p>When the woman returned, the watch was ticking softly on the counter. She held it to her ear, and her eyes filled with tears. \"I can hear my father again,\" she whispered. After she left, Sam's grandfather put a hand on Sam's shoulder and said, \"You saved that watch. Someday this shop will be yours.\" Sam smiled, already imagining the day <u>(e) he</u> would sit at the workbench himself.</p><p class='lab'>(D)</p><p>Sam thought for a moment and then remembered the box of broken watches under the counter. \"Grandpa, what about those?\" Sam asked. His grandfather glanced at the dusty box. At first <u>(c) he</u> looked doubtful, but then he nodded. Together they searched through dozens of old watches until, near midnight, they found a gear of exactly the right size. His grandfather held it up to the light, and for the first time in days, <u>(d) he</u> laughed.</p></div><p class='stem q2'>45. 윗글에 관한 내용으로 적절하지 않은 것은?</p><div class='ch long'><span>① Sam은 토요일마다 할아버지의 가게를 방문했다.</span><span>② 여자의 시계는 그녀 아버지의 것이었다.</span><span>③ 할아버지는 부러진 톱니를 새로 사서 교체했다.</span><span>④ 자정 무렵 크기가 맞는 톱니를 찾았다.</span><span>⑤ 여자는 시계 소리를 듣고 눈물을 글썽였다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "3", "anst": "③ 할아버지는 부러진 톱니를 새로 사서 교체했다.", "sol": "새 부품은 어디서도 살 수 없었고(no replacement could be bought), 고장 난 시계 상자에서 톱니를 찾았다.", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.135, "d": "lo"}, {"lv": 3, "q": "<p class='stem'>[43~45] 다음 글을 읽고, 물음에 답하시오.</p><div class='psg'><p class='lab'>(A)</p><p>When Hana was offered a seat in the city youth orchestra, she assumed it would be in the first violin section, where she had always played. Instead, the conductor, Ms. Ortiz, placed her among the second violins, a section whose parts were mostly accompaniment. Hana accepted politely, but at home she told her mother that <u>(a) she</u> had been punished for something she did not understand.</p><p class='lab'>(B)</p><p>At the concert, Hana played the same notes she had played for weeks, but they no longer felt like the same notes. When the slow movement ended, the hall was silent for a moment before the applause began. Afterward, Ms. Ortiz found her backstage. \"Next season,\" <u>(c) she</u> said, \"I would like you to lead the second violins.\" Hana realized that <u>(d) she</u> was being offered not a consolation but a responsibility, and that <u>(e) she</u> wanted it.</p><p class='lab'>(C)</p><p>\"Neither line is the music,\" Ms. Ortiz said. \"The music is what happens between them.\" After rehearsal, she handed Hana a recording of a famous string quartet. \"Listen to the second violinist,\" she said, \"not the first.\" That night Hana listened four times. By the fourth, <u>(b) she</u> could hear how the second violin shaped every phrase the first violin played, leaning into a chord just before the melody rose and holding back so that the melody could breathe.</p><p class='lab'>(D)</p><p>At the first rehearsals, Hana played her part flawlessly but with little interest, her eyes drifting toward the first violins whenever they had the melody. Ms. Ortiz said nothing about it. Then, a week before the concert, she stopped the orchestra in the middle of a slow movement and asked the second violins to play their line alone. To Hana's surprise, the harmony she had been treating as background sounded thin and uncertain on its own, yet the melody, when the first violins tried it alone, sounded strangely empty too.</p></div><p class='stem q2'>45. 윗글에 관한 내용으로 적절하지 않은 것은?</p><div class='ch long'><span>① Hana는 이전에 늘 제1바이올린 파트에서 연주했다.</span><span>② Ms. Ortiz는 연습 초기에 Hana의 연주 태도를 지적했다.</span><span>③ 제2바이올린만 연주하자 화음이 빈약하게 들렸다.</span><span>④ Ms. Ortiz는 Hana에게 유명한 현악 사중주 녹음을 건넸다.</span><span>⑤ Ms. Ortiz는 다음 시즌에 제2바이올린을 이끌어 달라고 했다.</span></div>", "mc": true, "ans": "2", "anst": "② Ms. Ortiz는 연습 초기에 Hana의 연주 태도를 지적했다.", "sol": "Ms. Ortiz는 Hana의 무심한 태도에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았다(said nothing about it).", "note": "새로 쓴 지문", "f": ["ko"], "r": 0.45, "d": "mid"}]};
